        "question_no": "Q1",
        "question": "A nightly JDBC extract of a 60-million-row table has started to slow the source OLTP database during hours when it is serving business-critical transactions. The DBA will not allow the extract window to move. Which change most directly addresses the DBA's concern?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "Understanding the Problem\nThe extract is a large analytical read against a database serving transactions. It competes for CPU, I/O, buffer cache, and possibly locks. The DBA's objection is legitimate and the fix must reduce load on the primary \u2014 no Spark-side tuning can help, because the work is happening in the database.\nRead Replica vs Standby\nThese sound interchangeable and are not:\nRead replica \u2014 a copy kept in sync and available to serve queries. It exists precisely to absorb read workloads that would otherwise contend with the primary. Slight replication lag is expected and acceptable.\nStandby \u2014 a copy kept in sync purely so it can take over on failover. It is not available for queries while standing by; keeping it idle is the point, since it must be ready to assume the primary role instantly.\nWhy the Replica Works Here\nA nightly extract has no need for data current to the millisecond. Replication lag of seconds is invisible to a batch load. So moving the extract to the replica removes contention entirely without changing the schedule \u2014 which is what the DBA required.\nComplementary Improvements\nWhile at it: use partitionColumn, lowerBound, upperBound, and numPartitions to parallelize the read (default JDBC reads use a single thread), and switch to incremental extraction on a last_modified watermark so the extract reads only changed rows rather than all 60 million.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nSwitch to Auto Loader \u2014 reads files from cloud object storage; it has no JDBC client. There is also no way to extract rows from a live database without querying it somehow.\nIncrease spark.sql.shuffle.partitions \u2014 governs how Spark redistributes data inside its own cluster after reading. It has no bearing on the work the source database performs.\nPoint at the standby \u2014 the trap. A standby is not available to serve queries; if it were, it wouldn't be a standby. The read replica is the copy intended to be queried.\nKey Takeaway\nMove analytical extracts to a read replica to remove contention with transactional traffic. A standby exists for failover and is not queryable. Spark-side settings cannot reduce load on the source.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Query databases using JDBC \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/connect/external-systems/jdbc\nDatabricks \u2014 What is query federation? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/query-federation/database-federation\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/",
        "optionA": "Point the extract at the source's standby database, which is kept fully in sync and sits idle until a failover occurs",
