        "question_no": "Q2",
        "question": "A platform team wants OPTIMIZE, VACUUM, and statistics maintenance to happen across hundreds of Unity Catalog managed tables, without scheduling and tuning a maintenance job per table. Which capability addresses this?",
        "Correct answer": "optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests predictive optimization \u2014 automated table maintenance, and the answer to a problem that appears the moment an estate grows past a few dozen tables.\nWhy Manual Maintenance Doesn't Scale\nEach table needs OPTIMIZE to compact files, VACUUM to reclaim storage, and ANALYZE to collect statistics. Hand-managing that means a scheduled job per table, each with a cadence someone guessed at.\nAt hundreds of tables the guesses go wrong in both directions: heavily written tables are under-maintained and degrade, quiet tables are over-maintained and burn compute on nothing. Nobody has time to tune them individually.\nWhat Predictive Optimization Does\nDatabricks observes how each Unity Catalog managed table is written and queried, and decides when maintenance is worth running:\nOPTIMIZE \u2014 compacts file sizes and incrementally clusters new data\nVACUUM \u2014 deletes unused files to reduce storage cost\nANALYZE \u2014 collects statistics that enable data skipping\nMaintenance follows actual usage rather than a fixed schedule. It's enabled by default for accounts created on or after 11 November 2024, and Databricks has been gradually enabling it for existing accounts.\nThe Managed Table Connection\nThis is one of the concrete reasons Databricks recommends managed tables. Predictive optimization requires Unity Catalog to own the storage lifecycle, so it applies to managed tables \u2014 not external or foreign ones. It also underpins automatic liquid clustering, where clustering keys are selected and updated as query patterns change.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nAuto Loader \u2014 ingests files into tables. It does not compact the target, and frequent streaming appends are a common cause of the small-file problem maintenance resolves.\nLakeflow Job with a table update trigger \u2014 hand-built maintenance jobs are possible and were the norm before predictive optimization, but they are neither the only option nor a good fit at this scale. A table update trigger also fires on data changes rather than on a maintenance need.\nDeletion vectors \u2014 make deletes and updates cheaper by deferring file rewrites, which increases the need for later maintenance rather than removing it.\nKey Takeaway\nPredictive optimization automatically runs OPTIMIZE, VACUUM, and ANALYZE on Unity Catalog managed tables, deciding when based on observed usage \u2014 removing the need for per-table maintenance jobs.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Predictive optimization \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/predictive-optimization\nDatabricks \u2014 Unity Catalog managed tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/managed\nDatabricks \u2014 Use liquid clustering for tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/clustering",
        "optionA": "Deletion vectors, which remove the need for maintenance by keeping files permanently well organized",
