        "question_no": "Q3",
        "question": "A team is moving from manually created jobs to Declarative Automation Bundles. Which TWO statements about what a bundle manages are correct? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionA, optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests what a bundle actually manages \u2014 the two properties that make it a deployment mechanism rather than a configuration file.\nProperty 1 \u2014 Code and Resources Deploy Together\nA bundle defines Lakeflow Jobs, pipelines, and the notebooks and source files they run, deploying them as a single unit.\nThat coupling is the point. A deployed job always runs the code version that shipped with it. If notebooks were synced separately, a job could silently run code nobody deployed \u2014 someone edits a notebook in the workspace and the next scheduled run behaves differently with no deployment record. Shipping them together removes that possibility.\nProperty 2 \u2014 Deployments Reconcile State\nResources are tracked as bundle-managed, so a later deploy makes the workspace match the configuration. Remove a job from the configuration, redeploy, and it is removed from the workspace.\nThis is what makes a bundle declarative rather than a deployment script. A script that only creates and updates leaves abandoned jobs behind after every refactor \u2014 jobs nobody dares delete because nobody is certain they're unused. State tracking eliminates that accumulation.\nWhy These Two Together\nThey combine into the useful guarantee: the workspace reflects the configuration, and the configuration is in Git. So the repository is the source of truth for what exists and what it runs \u2014 which is exactly why a manual edit in the workspace is overwritten at the next deploy.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Removes the need for Git\" \u2014 a databricks.yml is source code and belongs in Git like any other file. It carries no history of its own; Git is what versions it, and the bundle even supports Git settings in its configuration.\n\"Synchronizes Unity Catalog table contents between targets\" \u2014 bundles deploy code and resource definitions, never data. Copying production data into lower environments would be a governance and privacy problem, which is why targets normally point at different catalogs.\n\"Deploys jobs but not pipelines\" \u2014 pipelines are a first-class bundle resource. Being able to promote a pipeline through dev, test, and prod is one of the primary reasons to adopt bundles.\nKey Takeaway\nA bundle deploys jobs, pipelines, and the code they run as one unit, and tracks what it created so a later deploy reconciles the workspace to the configuration \u2014 including removing resources you deleted.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Declarative Automation Bundles resources \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/resources\nDatabricks \u2014 Declarative Automation Bundles configuration \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/settings\nDatabricks \u2014 CI/CD on Databricks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/ci-cd-bundles",
