        "question_no": "Q4",
        "question": "A six-task job fails on task five, after the first four tasks completed successfully and took about 20 minutes each. The defect is corrected in the notebook. The team wants to complete the run without repeating the successful work. What should they do?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question tests repair run \u2014 the recovery mechanism that makes multi-task jobs practical.\nWhat Repair Run Does\nRather than starting over, a repair run re-executes only the failed task and the tasks downstream of it, reusing the results of tasks that already succeeded.\nHere that means tasks 1\u20134 are not repeated. Only task 5 and task 6 run. Roughly 80 minutes of compute is saved, and \u2014 often more importantly \u2014 the recovery completes 80 minutes sooner, which matters when a delivery deadline is approaching.\nWhy It's Recorded Against the Original Run\nThe repair attaches to the run that failed rather than creating a separate one. The run history shows one logical execution with a repair attempt, so anyone reviewing later sees both the failure and its resolution together.\nFire a fresh run instead and the history shows a failed run and an unrelated successful one, with nothing linking them. Reconstructing what happened during an incident becomes guesswork.\nWhy Multi-Task Design Enables This\nThe pattern rewards decomposing work into discrete tasks. A pipeline written as one monolithic notebook has nothing to repair \u2014 failure at 95% means rerunning from zero. Six tasks means failure at task five costs task five onward.\nThis is the same reasoning behind preferring three parameterized notebook tasks over one notebook looping internally, and behind for-each tasks over in-notebook loops.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Restart the job cluster\" \u2014 a cluster restart affects compute, not run state. The run has already terminated in a failed state; restarting its cluster doesn't resume it.\n\"Temporary job with tasks five and six\" \u2014 duplicates task definitions and dependencies, and records the work outside the original job's history. The run that matters still shows as failed with no explanation of how it was resolved. It also has to be remembered and deleted.\n\"New run \u2014 a failed run cannot be resumed\" \u2014 the premise is false, and the reasoning is too: there's no reason tasks 1\u20134 would run faster the second time. This wastes 80 minutes for nothing.\nKey Takeaway\nA repair run re-executes only the failed task and its downstream dependents, reusing successful results and recording the fix against the original run. Decomposing work into discrete tasks is what makes this possible.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/\nDatabricks \u2014 Configure and edit Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/configure-job\nDatabricks \u2014 Jobs system table reference \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/jobs",
        "optionA": "Trigger a repair run, which reruns only the failed task and the tasks downstream of it, reusing the results of the tasks that already succeeded",
        "optionB": "Build a temporary job containing only tasks five and six, run that, and then delete it",
        "optionC": "Trigger a new run of the job, since a failed run cannot be resumed and the first four tasks will complete more quickly the second time",
