        "question": "A team ingests roughly 400,000 small JSON files per day into a Bronze table, arriving continuously throughout the day. The source schema changes occasionally. They must choose between COPY INTO and Auto Loader. Which choice fits, and why?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question is the mirror image of the COPY INTO scenario elsewhere in the set. Same two tools, opposite workload \u2014 and the workload decides.\nReading the Three Signals\n400,000 files per day \u2014 very high file count. Discovery and tracking must scale.\nContinuous arrival \u2014 data flows all day, not in a scheduled drop.\nSchema changes occasionally \u2014 the pipeline must adapt without manual intervention.\nEvery signal points to Auto Loader.\nWhy Auto Loader Fits Each\nScale \u2014 the checkpoint is built to track millions of files efficiently, and discovery can move from directory listing to file notification when listing becomes the bottleneck. At 400,000 files a day, that ceiling arrives quickly.\nContinuous arrival \u2014 Auto Loader is a streaming source. It runs continuously or on a short trigger, processing files as they land rather than waiting for someone to invoke a command.\nSchema changes \u2014 schema inference, schema hints, evolution modes, and the rescued data column give a documented answer for each kind of change. COPY INTO offers far less here.\nThe Decision Framework\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"COPY INTO \u2014 Auto Loader can't read JSON without an explicit schema\" \u2014 false. Auto Loader infers JSON schemas, and that inference is one reason it suits this source.\n\"Auto Loader, but consolidate files first \u2014 it can't handle more than a few thousand\" \u2014 handling very large file counts is a core design goal. File notification mode exists for exactly this scale. Consolidation may help query performance later; it is not a prerequisite.\n\"Either \u2014 functionally equivalent, differ only in syntax\" \u2014 they differ in execution model, tracking mechanism, scale characteristics, and schema handling. Choosing on syntax would ignore everything that matters.\n\"COPY INTO \u2014 file-level idempotency scales better\" \u2014 COPY INTO's tracking is effective for periodic bulk loads but was not built for hundreds of thousands of files arriving continuously. The streaming checkpoint is what scales here.\nKey Takeaway\nHigh volume + continuous arrival + evolving schema \u2192 Auto Loader. Modest, periodic, stable deliveries \u2192 COPY INTO. Read the workload rather than defaulting to one tool.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Auto Loader? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader\nDatabricks \u2014 Get started using COPY INTO to load data \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/copy-into\nDatabricks \u2014 Set up incremental ingestion from Amazon S3 \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/onboard-data",
