        "question": "A team notices that when a bundle is deployed to the dev target, the resulting job appears in the workspace with a name prefixed by the developer's username and with its schedule paused. Neither happens in prod. What explains this?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests bundle deployment modes \u2014 a behavior that looks like a bug the first time you encounter it and is actually a deliberate safety feature.\nThe Two Modes\nDevelopment mode \u2014 intended for a shared workspace where several engineers deploy the same bundle. It applies two transformations:\nName prefixing. Deployed resources are prefixed with the deploying user's identity. Without this, two engineers deploying the same bundle would both create a job called nightly_etl \u2014 and the second deployment would silently overwrite the first. Prefixing gives each developer their own copy.\nSchedule pausing. Deployed schedules are paused. Half-finished work should not start running on a timer overnight, and three developers' copies of the same job should not all fire at 2 a.m. against a dev catalog.\nProduction mode \u2014 neither applies. A job carries its real name and honors its schedule, because that's the whole point of deploying it.\nWhy This Is the Right Default\nBoth behaviors solve problems that only appear with more than one developer, which is why they can be surprising to the first person who hits them. The alternative \u2014 dev deployments colliding and firing unattended \u2014 is far worse than a prefixed name.\nRecognizing the Symptom\nPrefixed name plus paused schedule in dev, normal in prod, is a distinctive signature. Once you know it, it identifies development mode immediately rather than sending you looking for a configuration error.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Failed to resolve variable overrides, fell back to placeholders\" \u2014 an unresolved variable causes deployment to fail with an error, not to substitute a placeholder. A username prefix is also a deliberate, recognizable pattern rather than a fallback value.\n\"Workspace naming policy for non-production jobs\" \u2014 the behavior comes from the bundle's target configuration, not a workspace-level rule. A job created by hand in the same workspace would not be renamed.\n\"The deploying identity type determines naming\" \u2014 the deploying identity governs what the deployment may do and who owns the result. It has no effect on resource naming or schedule state. (The run-as identity is a separate setting again \u2014 worth keeping distinct.)\nKey Takeaway\nDevelopment mode prefixes deployed resource names with the deploying user and pauses schedules, so several developers can deploy the same bundle without colliding or triggering unattended runs. Production mode does neither.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Declarative Automation Bundles configuration \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/settings\nDatabricks \u2014 CI/CD on Databricks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/ci-cd-bundles\nDatabricks \u2014 Declarative Automation Bundles FAQs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/faqs",
