        "question_no": "Q7",
        "question": "An engineer must determine which downstream tables and dashboards would be affected if a column were removed from a Silver table. Which Unity Catalog capability answers this, and what is its scope?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests data lineage \u2014 and specifically the impact analysis it makes possible.\nThe Question Being Asked\n\"What breaks if I remove this column?\" is one of the most common and most dreaded questions in data engineering. Without lineage the honest answer is I don't know \u2014 followed by grep, guesswork, and a change made hopefully.\nWhat Lineage Provides\nAutomatic capture. Lineage is recorded as a by-product of query execution against Unity Catalog objects. Nobody declares it, so it reflects what actually happens rather than what someone documented and then forgot to update.\nColumn-level granularity. This is what makes it usable here. Table-level lineage would say \"these five tables read from Silver\" \u2014 most of which may not touch the column in question. Column-level lineage shows which downstream objects depend on that specific column, turning a broad worry into a precise list.\nTwo access paths. Visual exploration in Catalog Explorer for ad-hoc investigation, and system tables for programmatic queries \u2014 so an impact check can be scripted or wired into a CI job that flags breaking schema changes before merge.\nIts Boundaries\nLineage captures what runs through Unity Catalog. Consumers reaching data by other routes \u2014 an external tool reading files directly, an application with its own copy \u2014 sit outside it. Worth knowing rather than assuming complete coverage.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Lineage must be declared by hand\" \u2014 manual lineage rots immediately, since it depends on every engineer updating it whenever a query changes. Automatic capture is the feature's central value.\n\"Audit logs \u2014 reconstruct dependencies from query text\" \u2014 audit records exist and do contain query information, but they are built for security and compliance: who did what, when. Parsing SQL text to reconstruct a dependency graph would be laborious and unreliable, and it's what lineage already does properly.\n\"DESCRIBE HISTORY lists downstream objects\" \u2014 reports the operations that changed a table: writes, merges, optimizations. It looks backward at the table's own versions and says nothing about who reads from it.\nKey Takeaway\nData lineage is captured automatically for queries against Unity Catalog objects, includes column-level relationships, and is available in Catalog Explorer or through system tables \u2014 making impact analysis precise rather than speculative.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Unity Catalog? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/\nDatabricks \u2014 Manage privileges in Unity Catalog \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges\nDatabricks \u2014 Jobs system table reference \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/system-tables/jobs",
