        "question_no": "Q8",
        "question": "A long-running write to a Delta table fails partway through because the cluster loses a worker. While the failure is being cleaned up, another user queries the same table. What does that user see?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question tests atomicity \u2014 the A in ACID \u2014 and it is arguably the single most important property Delta Lake provides.\nHow a Delta Write Actually Works\nA write proceeds in two phases:\nPhase 1 \u2014 stage the data. New Parquet files are written to the table's directory. At this point they exist on storage but are not part of the table, because nothing references them.\nPhase 2 \u2014 commit. A single entry is appended to the transaction log recording which files were added and removed.\nThe table is defined by its log, not by the contents of its directory. A file that no commit references does not exist as far as any reader is concerned.\nApplying This to the Failure\nThe cluster lost a worker mid-write, so phase 2 never happened. Some Parquet files may be sitting in the directory, but no commit points at them.\nA concurrent reader resolves the table through the log, sees the last committed version, and gets a complete, consistent snapshot. It does not know or care that a failed write left files behind. Those orphans are later cleaned up by VACUUM.\nWhy Readers Aren't Blocked\nDelta uses optimistic concurrency with snapshot isolation rather than reader-blocking locks. Every reader works against a specific committed version, so a writer never makes readers wait \u2014 and a reader never sees a version that is partially applied.\nWhy This Matters Practically\nThis is what makes a lakehouse usable for concurrent workloads. On a plain Parquet data lake, a reader listing the directory during a failed write sees whatever files happen to be there \u2014 a half-written result presented as if it were complete. Delta's log is what removes that failure mode.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"An empty result; the table is in an indeterminate state\" \u2014 the previous version remains fully intact and readable. A failed write neither empties the table nor requires repair before it can be queried.\n\"An error; the table is locked during any write\" \u2014 Delta takes no reader-blocking locks. Readers proceeding unimpeded during writes is a design goal.\n\"The rows written before the failure\" \u2014 this describes progressive commits, which is what a data lake without a transaction log does, and precisely the behavior Delta exists to prevent.\nKey Takeaway\nA Delta write becomes visible only when its transaction commits. A partial write is never readable \u2014 concurrent readers continue to see the last committed version, and orphaned files are cleaned up by VACUUM.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Delta Lake in Databricks? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta\nDatabricks \u2014 Work with table history \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/history\nDatabricks \u2014 Remove unused data files with vacuum \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/operations/vacuum",
        "optionA": "The last successfully committed version of the table, because a Delta write becomes visible only when its transaction commits - a partial write is never readable",
        "optionB": "An empty result, because a failed write leaves the table in an indeterminate state until it is repaired",
        "optionC": "An error, because the table is locked for the duration of any write",
        "optionD": "The rows written before the failure, because Delta commits data files progressively as they are produced",
        "answer": [
            "optionA"
        ],
