        "question_no": "Q9",
        "question": "A table is defined as:\nCREATE TABLE users (\nid BIGINT,\nbirthday DATE,\nbirth_year INT GENERATED ALWAYS AS (YEAR(birthday))\n);\nAn engineer attempts an INSERT that supplies values for id, birthday, and birth_year. What happens?",
        "Correct answer": "optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests generated columns \u2014 values Delta computes rather than accepts.\nWhat a Generated Column Is\nbirth_year INT GENERATED ALWAYS AS (YEAR(birthday))\nGENERATED ALWAYS AS declares that Delta computes this column's value from an expression over other columns, on write, and persists the result.\nThe point is a guarantee: birth_year can never disagree with birthday, because nothing else is permitted to set it.\nWhy the Insert Is Rejected\nSupplying an explicit birth_year asks Delta to accept a value it is responsible for computing. If that value differed from YEAR(birthday), the column's entire purpose would be void \u2014 a derived column that isn't reliably derived is worse than no column at all, because downstream logic trusts it.\nSo the write is rejected. The correct insert simply omits the column:\nINSERT INTO users (id, birthday) VALUES (1, '1990-04-12');\nDelta computes birth_year as 1990.\nNote the nuance in the option's wording: unless the supplied value matches what the expression produces. Delta permits a value that agrees with the computation \u2014 it rejects disagreement, not participation.\nWhy Generated Columns Are Useful\nBeyond consistency, they help performance. A generated column derived from a timestamp is a common way to give queries a column that supports data skipping, and Delta can sometimes use the generating expression to prune files even when a query filters on the source column.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"The supplied value overrides the expression\" \u2014 this would let birth_year contradict birthday, defeating the guarantee the column exists to provide.\n\"The generated expression is dropped from the table\" \u2014 a DML statement never alters a table's schema. Converting a generated column to an ordinary one requires a deliberate DDL change.\n\"birth_year is left null; generated columns evaluate at read time\" \u2014 this definition specifies generation on write, with the value persisted. Leaving it null would give downstream queries nothing to read.\nKey Takeaway\nA GENERATED ALWAYS AS column is computed by Delta on write and persisted. Omit it from inserts \u2014 a supplied value is rejected unless it matches what the expression produces.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Delta Lake generated columns \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/features/generated-columns\nDatabricks \u2014 Constraints on Databricks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/constraints\nDatabricks \u2014 Data skipping \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/data-skipping",
