        "question_no": "Q11",
        "question": "A bundle-deployed job needs a password in order to reach an external database. The engineer must keep the value out of the repository. Which approach is appropriate?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests secret management \u2014 keeping credentials out of source control while remaining usable by automated jobs.\nHow Secret Scopes Work\nA secret scope is a named collection of secrets, stored in an encrypted database owned and managed by Databricks. A notebook retrieves a value by naming the scope and key:\npassword = dbutils.secrets.get(scope=\"jdbc\", key=\"password\")\nThe repository contains only the scope name and key name \u2014 never the value.\nThe Four Properties That Matter\nNothing sensitive in Git. The value never enters the repository, so it never enters its permanent history.\nRotation without a code change. Update the secret in the scope; every job picks up the new value on its next run. No commit, no deployment, no coordination.\nAccess control. Scopes have their own permissions. By default the creator receives MANAGE, allowing them to read, write, and manage permissions on the scope. A job's service principal is granted read access explicitly \u2014 so a credential is available only to the workloads that need it.\nRedaction. Values retrieved through the secrets utility are redacted in notebook output and logs. This is the safety net for the most common accident: printing a variable or logging a connection string. Without it, one careless print() puts the credential into job logs where it persists.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nCluster environment variable via the UI \u2014 keeps the value out of Git, which is only half the requirement. It is readable by anyone who can view the compute configuration or print the environment from a notebook, so it is not a secure store. It also has to be reconfigured on every cluster.\nPaste into a cell and clear before committing \u2014 depends on a human remembering, every time, under deadline pressure. One missed clear and the secret is in the repository's history permanently \u2014 clearing the cell afterward does not remove it from past commits.\nBundle variable with a default \u2014 a databricks.yml is plain text committed to the repository, so a default value is committed like any other line. Whether the platform encrypts stored configuration is beside the point: the secret is already in Git.\nKey Takeaway\nStore credentials in a Databricks secret scope and read them with dbutils.secrets.get. The repository holds only scope and key names, values are redacted in output and logs, scopes have their own access controls, and rotation requires no code change.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Secret management \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/security/secrets/\nDatabricks \u2014 Tutorial: Create and use a Databricks secret \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/security/secrets/example-secret-workflow\nDatabricks \u2014 Query databases using JDBC \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/connect/external-systems/jdbc",
        "optionA": "Store the value in the bundle configuration as a variable with a default, since bundle variables are encrypted at rest",
        "optionB": "Paste the value into a notebook cell and clear the cell before each commit, so the plaintext never reaches the remote",
        "optionC": "Type the value into the compute UI as a cluster environment variable, since values entered in the UI are excluded from Git",
        "optionD": "Store the value in a Databricks secret scope and read it in the notebook with dbutils.secrets.get, so the repository contains only the scope and key names",
        "answer": [
            "optionD"
        ],
