        "question_no": "Q12",
        "question": "A declarative pipeline notebook defines three datasets, in this order in the file: gold_summary, bronze_raw, silver_clean. gold_summary reads silver_clean, and silver_clean reads bronze_raw. In what order does the pipeline execute them?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question tests what declarative actually means: you describe relationships, and the platform derives execution order.\nHow the Pipeline Builds the Graph\nEach dataset declares what it reads. The pipeline parses those references and constructs a dependency graph:\nbronze_raw \u2192 silver_clean \u2192 gold_summary\nExecution follows the graph, not the file. bronze_raw first, then silver_clean, then gold_summary \u2014 regardless of the order they appear in the source.\nWhy This Design Is Right\nAuthor for readability. Define datasets in whatever order reads most clearly \u2014 some teams put Gold first to show intent before implementation. Reordering never changes behavior.\nRefactoring is safe. Moving a definition can't break the pipeline, because ordering was never positional.\nParallelism comes free. Because the pipeline understands the graph, independent branches run concurrently without anyone declaring that they can.\nCorrectness by construction. The order is derived from actual data dependencies, so it cannot drift out of sync with the code the way a hand-maintained sequence would.\nThe Contrast Worth Drawing\nThis is the difference between a declarative pipeline and an imperative job. In a Lakeflow Job you state dependencies explicitly by wiring tasks together. In a declarative pipeline you state what each dataset reads, and the dependencies are inferred. Both produce a DAG; they differ in who constructs it.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Order they are defined\" \u2014 this would make the pipeline imperative rather than declarative, and would break the moment someone reordered the file for readability.\n\"All three concurrently\" \u2014 the datasets are explicitly dependent, so running them together would let gold_summary read a silver_clean that doesn't yet exist. Parallelism applies to independent datasets, not to a chain.\n\"Fails validation \u2014 a dataset may not be defined before its dependencies\" \u2014 no such requirement exists, and enforcing one would be a pointless constraint given the graph is inferred from references.\nKey Takeaway\nA declarative pipeline infers execution order from the references between datasets, not from their order in the file. Author for readability; the pipeline derives the DAG and parallelizes independent branches.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow pipelines \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp\nDatabricks \u2014 Streaming tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/streaming-tables\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/",
        "optionA": "bronze_raw, then silver_clean, then gold_summary - the pipeline infers dependencies from the references between datasets rather than from their order in the file",
        "optionB": "The pipeline fails validation, because a dataset may not be defined before the datasets it depends on",
        "optionC": "gold_summary, then bronze_raw, then silver_clean - datasets execute in the order they are defined",
        "optionD": "All three concurrently, because declarative datasets are independent by definition and are materialized in parallel",
