        "question_no": "Q14",
        "question": "A catalog-level SELECT grant on catalog prod gives the analysts group access to every schema and table beneath it. One table, prod.hr.salaries, must not be readable by that group. Which TWO statements are correct? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionA, optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests privilege inheritance \u2014 and specifically that a broad grant cannot be selectively withdrawn lower down.\nThe Trap: REVOKE Doesn't Work Downward\nREVOKE removes a grant made at the level you name. The grant here lives on the catalog, so revoking at the table level removes nothing.\nWhat makes this genuinely dangerous is the failure mode: the command succeeds without error and access continues. An engineer runs it, sees no error, records the table as protected, and moves on. The gap is discovered during an audit, or not at all.\nThe Two Approaches That Work\nGrant narrowly in the first place. Replace the catalog-wide grant with schema- or table-level grants covering everything except prod.hr.salaries. More grants to maintain, but each says exactly what it means, and there is no inherited privilege to fight.\nSeparate by placement. Move sensitive data into a schema or catalog the group was never granted on. No exception is required, because the default is no access.\nThe second is the design lesson. Sensitive data is commonly separated at the schema or catalog boundary from the outset \u2014 an HR schema that analysts simply aren't granted on needs no carve-out, no exception to remember, and no risk of a later broad grant silently re-exposing it.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"REVOKE at table level removes the inherited privilege\" \u2014 the central trap, addressed above. The privilege lives on the catalog; the command succeeds and changes nothing.\n\"Column mask on every column removes the ability to query\" \u2014 a mask changes the values returned, not access. The group could still query the table, count rows, see which employees exist, and read anything unmasked. Masking is fine-grained control over content; it is not a substitute for withholding access.\n\"Changing the owner withdraws inherited SELECT\" \u2014 ownership confers administrative rights over an object: altering it, dropping it, managing its grants. It does not withdraw privileges other principals hold or inherit.\nKey Takeaway\nPrivileges flow downward and cannot be selectively revoked lower down \u2014 REVOKE at the table level removes nothing when the grant is on the catalog. Grant narrowly, or separate sensitive data by schema or catalog from the outset.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Manage privileges in Unity Catalog \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Unity Catalog? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/\nDatabricks \u2014 Row filters and column masks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/",
        "optionA": "Relocating the sensitive table into a schema or catalog the group is not granted on isolates it cleanly, which is why sensitive data is commonly separated at the schema level from the outset",
        "optionB": "Applying a column mask to every column of prod.hr.salaries removes the group's ability to query the table at all",
        "optionC": "Changing the table's owner to a different group withdraws the analysts group's inherited SELECT privilege",
        "optionD": "Replacing the broad grant with narrower ones - SELECT at the schema or table level everywhere except prod.hr.salaries - achieves the requirement, at the cost of more grants to maintain",
        "optionE": "REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE prod.hr.salaries FROM analysts removes the inherited privilege for that one table while leaving the rest of the catalog grant in place",
