        "question_no": "Q18",
        "question": "A pipeline update fails and the team must investigate. Which TWO kinds of information does the pipeline's event log provide? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionC, optionE",
        "explanation": "This question tests the pipeline event log \u2014 the operational record of what a pipeline did.\nWhat the Event Log Records\nDataset-level state changes and error details. Which datasets initialized, updated, completed, or failed, and for a failure, what the error was and which dataset produced it. This answers the first question after any failure \u2014 what broke, and where \u2014 without opening notebooks and tracing dependencies by hand.\nData quality metrics per expectation. Every expectation's results are recorded as structured metrics: how many records passed, how many were dropped or failed, per rule, per update.\nWhy the Metrics Matter More Than They Appear\nBecause expectation results are recorded per rule per update, the event log doubles as a data quality trend. A rule that normally drops 3 records suddenly dropping 40,000 is visible immediately \u2014 long before anyone questions a report.\nThis is why naming each expectation individually is worth the effort. Combine three conditions into one rule and you get a single count that hides which condition is failing.\nUsing It in Practice\nThe event log is queryable, so teams commonly build a dashboard over it: expectation pass rates over time, update durations, failure frequency by dataset. That turns pipeline health from something you check reactively into something you monitor.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Source system's own error logs, forwarded automatically\" \u2014 the event log covers what happens inside the pipeline. An external system's internal logs are outside its scope and must be consulted in that system.\n\"Cloud provider billing per dataset\" \u2014 cost attribution comes from billing and system tables. The event log records what the pipeline did, not what it cost.\n\"Spark physical plan for every query\" \u2014 physical plans are a query-tuning artifact belonging to the Spark UI. The event log operates at the level of datasets and updates rather than individual query execution.\nKey Takeaway\nThe pipeline event log records dataset-level state changes and error details, plus per-expectation data quality metrics for each update \u2014 making it both the first stop after a failure and a data quality trend over time.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow pipelines \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp\nDatabricks \u2014 Manage data quality with pipeline expectations \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations\nDatabricks \u2014 Use Unity Catalog with pipelines \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/unity-catalog",
        "optionA": "The cloud provider's billing charges attributable to each dataset in the pipeline",
