        "question_no": "Q24",
        "question": "An engineer wants to inspect a set of Parquet files sitting in cloud storage before deciding how to ingest them, without creating any table. Which approach works?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "This question tests querying files directly \u2014 the fastest way to look at raw data during exploration.\nThe Format Prefix Syntax\nsql\nSELECT * FROM parquet.`s3://bucket/events/`\nTwo elements:\nThe format prefix \u2014 parquet., json., csv., or delta. tells Spark which reader to use.\nThe path in backticks \u2014 backticks are required, because the path contains characters SQL would otherwise treat as operators.\nPointing at a directory reads every file beneath it as one dataset, so a single query samples the whole drop rather than one arbitrary file.\nWhy This Is the Right Tool Here\nThe engineer hasn't decided how to ingest yet, so creating a table would be premature \u2014 an object to name, govern, and clean up, describing data whose shape is still unknown. Direct path querying answers the questions that inform the decision: what columns exist, what the types look like, how many rows, whether there are surprises.\nUseful during exploration, and equally useful as a debugging step when an ingestion pipeline produces unexpected results and you want to see the raw source.\nWhy It's Exploration-Only\nPath-based access bypasses table-level governance, so it belongs in investigation rather than production pipelines. Note also that path-based access to Unity Catalog managed tables is not supported, because it would bypass access controls. For governed data, use table names.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nSELECT * FROM 's3://bucket/events/' \u2014 a bare string in the FROM clause is not valid syntax, and Spark does not infer the reader from file extensions. The format prefix exists precisely because the format must be stated.\nCopy into a volume first \u2014 any governed storage path is queryable, whether reached through an external location or a volume. Copying 900 GB before looking at it also defeats the purpose of a quick inspection.\nCreate an external table first \u2014 the premise is false, and it means registering an object for data you haven't yet decided how to handle, leaving something to clean up afterward.\nKey Takeaway\nQuery files directly with a format prefix and a backtick-quoted path \u2014 SELECT * FROM parquet.\\s3://...`` \u2014 reading an entire directory. Ideal for exploration and debugging; use table names for governed production access.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Get started using COPY INTO to load data \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/copy-into\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Auto Loader? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader\nDatabricks \u2014 Unity Catalog managed tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/managed",
        "optionA": "Query the path as SELECT * FROM 's3://bucket/events/' and let Spark infer the format from the file extensions",
        "optionB": "Query the path directly using a format prefix - for example SELECT * FROM parquet.`s3://bucket/events/` - which reads every file in that directory",
        "optionC": "Copy the files into a Unity Catalog volume first, because only volume paths can be queried from SQL",
        "optionD": "Create an external table over the path first, because a storage path cannot be referenced in a FROM clause",
