        "question_no": "Q33",
        "question": "A team is evaluating Photon for an ETL workload. Which statement about Photon is correct?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "What Photon Is\nA vectorized query engine written in native C++ rather than running on the JVM. Rather than processing row by row, it processes batches of thousands of rows using SIMD instructions, with columnar batch processing enabling cache-friendly sequential reads. Running natively also eliminates garbage collection pauses and JIT warm-up.\nIt plugs into the Databricks Runtime as an execution engine \u2014 it does not replace the runtime, and it is not a compute type.\nWhere It's Enabled\nOn by default on SQL warehouses and serverless compute, and available as a checkbox on classic compute (enabled by default from Runtime 9.1 LTS onward).\nThe Limit That Matters Most\nPhoton accelerates operations expressed through Spark SQL and the DataFrame API \u2014 scans, filters, joins, aggregations, and Delta and Parquet writes.\nIt does not accelerate arbitrary Python or Scala. A Python UDF still runs in a Python process outside the engine, with the same serialization boundary, and Photon cannot see inside it. A pipeline dominated by Python UDFs sees little benefit \u2014 which is a strong argument for expressing logic with built-in functions wherever possible.\nHow It Lowers Cost\nPhoton-enabled compute carries a higher DBU rate. The saving comes from finishing sooner: a workload completing in a third of the time at 1.5\u00d7 the rate costs less overall. That's why the framing is \"lower total cost per workload\" rather than \"cheaper compute.\"\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Replaces the Databricks Runtime\" \u2014 Photon runs within the runtime, and a runtime version is still selected.\n\"A separate compute type\" \u2014 it's an option on compute. You still choose all-purpose or jobs compute, then enable Photon on it.\n\"Reduces DBU consumption to zero for the accelerated portion\" \u2014 no portion is free. The rate is higher, not zero, and the benefit is elapsed time.\n\"Accelerates arbitrary Python and Scala including UDFs\" \u2014 the single most important misconception to correct. Photon's scope is Spark SQL and DataFrame operations.\nKey Takeaway\nPhoton is a vectorized C++ query engine accelerating SQL and DataFrame operations, on by default for SQL warehouses and serverless compute. It does not accelerate Python UDFs or non-Spark code, and it lowers total cost by finishing faster despite a higher DBU rate.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Photon? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/photon\nDatabricks \u2014 Compute \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/\nDatabricks \u2014 Compute selection recommendations \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/choose-compute",
        "optionA": "Photon reduces DBU consumption to zero for the accelerated portion of a workload, which is why it lowers total cost",
        "optionB": "Photon is a separate compute type that must be provisioned in place of an all-purpose or job cluster",
