        "question_no": "Q38",
        "question": "A Bronze Delta table is appended to continuously by an Auto Loader stream. A Silver job must process each new Bronze row exactly once, incrementally, without rescanning the whole table on every run. Which approach fits?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question tests Delta tables as streaming sources \u2014 the mechanism that chains medallion layers incrementally.\nDelta Is a First-Class Streaming Source\nBecause every write to a Delta table produces a numbered commit in the transaction log, that log is itself an ordered stream of changes. spark.readStream.table('bronze.events') treats new commits as new data.\nThe Silver query maintains its own checkpoint, independent of the Auto Loader stream feeding Bronze. Each layer tracks its own progress:\nfiles \u2192 [Auto Loader + checkpoint A] \u2192 Bronze\n\u2193\nBronze \u2192 [readStream + checkpoint B] \u2192 Silver\nEach processes exactly once, incrementally, and recovers independently.\nWhy This Is the Standard Pattern\nEvery requirement is met without custom bookkeeping. Exactly-once comes from the checkpoint plus atomic Delta commits. Incremental processing means Silver never rescans Bronze. And a Silver failure doesn't affect Bronze ingestion \u2014 restarting resumes from its own checkpoint.\nOne Caveat Worth Knowing\nBy default, streaming from a Delta table assumes the source is append-only. If Bronze receives updates or deletes, the stream errors \u2014 deliberately, since it cannot know how those should propagate. Options such as ignoreChanges and ignoreDeletes exist for that case, and an append-only Bronze is the reason the standard pattern works so cleanly.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Nightly batch reading the full table\" \u2014 the premise is false, and a full rescan is exactly the cost the requirement rules out.\n\"Second Auto Loader stream at Bronze's storage directory\" \u2014 a genuine anti-pattern worth recognizing. Reading a Delta table's Parquet files directly bypasses the transaction log, so the stream can pick up files from uncommitted writes or files a later version removed \u2014 producing results corresponding to no committed version of the table. Always read Delta through the table, never through its files.\n\"Time travel predicate against a manually tracked version\" \u2014 this reimplements checkpointing by hand. It can work, but the control table becomes state that must remain correct through every failure and restart, which is precisely what the built-in checkpoint already guarantees.\nKey Takeaway\nRead a Delta table as a stream with spark.readStream.table(...) \u2014 new commits become new data, and the query keeps its own checkpoint for exactly-once incremental processing. Never stream from a Delta table's underlying files.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Delta Lake table streaming reads and writes \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/delta-lake\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Auto Loader? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader\nDatabricks \u2014 Medallion architecture \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/medallion",
        "optionA": "Read the Bronze table as a stream with spark.readStream.table('bronze.events'), which treats new commits to the Delta table as a streaming source and tracks progress in its own checkpoint",
        "optionB": "Run a nightly batch that reads the full Bronze table and merges it into Silver, since a Delta table cannot serve as a streaming source",
        "optionC": "Point a second Auto Loader stream at the Bronze table's underlying storage directory, since cloudFiles is the only incremental source available",
        "optionD": "Query Bronze with a time travel predicate against the previous run's version, tracked by hand in a control table",
        "answer": [
            "optionA"
        ],
        "type": "single"
