        "question_no": "Q39",
        "question": "An engineer must confirm which cloud storage path a named external location points to, and which storage credential it uses. Which command returns this?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question tests the DESCRIBE family again, this time for a Unity Catalog governance object rather than a table.\nWhat an External Location Is\nAn external location pairs a cloud storage path with a storage credential that authorizes access to it. It is the object that lets Databricks reach customer-owned storage under governance \u2014 external tables and external volumes are defined over it, and privileges on it control who may create them.\nWhy This Command Exists\nThe question \u2014 which path does this location point to, and which credential does it use \u2014 comes up constantly during troubleshooting. An external table creation fails, and the cause is usually one of: the location doesn't cover the path, the credential lacks access, or the caller lacks privileges on the location. DESCRIBE EXTERNAL LOCATION answers the first two directly, returning the name, URL, storage credential, owner, and comment.\nThe Object-to-Command Map\nEach DESCRIBE variant targets a specific object type. Passing an external location to a table-oriented variant doesn't fail gracefully with a warning \u2014 it fails because the object types don't match.\nWorth internalizing as a set: table, external location, schema, catalog, function, and query each have their own variant, and the exam has been known to test precisely this mapping.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nDESCRIBE SCHEMA EXTENDED \u2014 describes a schema, returning its name, comment, owner, and managed location. A schema is a container for tables and volumes, not a reference to a storage path with a credential.\nDESCRIBE DETAIL \u2014 reports physical metadata about a Delta table: format, size, file count, location. It does not accept an external location.\nSHOW EXTERNAL LOCATIONS \u2014 a genuinely useful discovery command that lists the locations defined in the metastore. But listing what exists is not the same as describing one in detail, and it doesn't answer the credential question. This is the strongest distractor: a real command that solves a neighboring problem.\nDESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED \u2014 describes a table. An external location is not a table.\nKey Takeaway\nDESCRIBE EXTERNAL LOCATION <name> returns the location's URL, storage credential, and owner. Match the DESCRIBE variant to the object type \u2014 table, external location, schema, and catalog each have their own.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 SQL language reference \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Unity Catalog? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/\nDatabricks \u2014 External tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/external",
        "optionA": "DESCRIBE EXTERNAL LOCATION <name>",
