        "question_no": "Q40",
        "question": "A team enables the rescued data column on an Auto Loader stream. Which TWO statements about it are correct? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionA, optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests the rescued data column \u2014 Auto Loader's safety net against data loss from unexpected input.\nThe Two Things It Catches\nType mismatches. A column declared or inferred as DOUBLE receives \"N/A\". Rather than failing the batch or dropping the record, Auto Loader writes null in the typed column and preserves the original value as JSON in the rescue column.\nUnknown fields. The source adds a field the stream's schema doesn't include. Rather than discarding it, the value is preserved in the rescue column.\nBoth cases share a principle: never silently lose data. The record still lands, and what didn't fit is retained verbatim for investigation.\nWhy This Matters\nUpstream teams change things without telling you. Without a rescue column, the first sign is a downstream report with unexplained nulls, and by then the original values are gone. With one, you can query the rescue column and see exactly what arrived and when.\nA useful monitoring practice: periodically check for non-null rescue values. A sudden appearance usually means the source changed.\nWhere It Fits Alongside Schema Evolution\nThe two are complementary, not alternatives. Schema evolution decides how the schema changes over time; the rescue column catches whatever still doesn't fit. Running both is standard.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Automatically repairs mistyped values by casting\" \u2014 nothing is repaired. Guessing at the intended value of unparseable input would introduce silent errors, which is the opposite of the feature's purpose. The original text is preserved verbatim for a human to interpret.\n\"Enabling it disables schema evolution\" \u2014 they operate at different points and are commonly used together.\n\"Records excluded and written to a quarantine table\" \u2014 the record stays in the target table; only the problematic values are relocated into the rescue column. Routing whole records elsewhere is a pattern teams build with pipeline expectations, but it is not what this feature does.\nKey Takeaway\nThe rescued data column captures type mismatches and unknown fields, preserving the original value while the record still lands in the target. Nothing is repaired or quarantined, and it works alongside schema evolution.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Auto Loader? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader\nDatabricks \u2014 Schema enforcement \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/schema-enforcement\nDatabricks \u2014 Manage data quality with pipeline expectations \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations",
        "optionA": "Fields that appear in a source file but are absent from the stream's schema are also captured there, so unexpected source changes do not lose data",
        "optionB": "Enabling it disables schema evolution, because rescuing data and evolving the schema are mutually exclusive",
        "optionC": "Values that do not match the declared or inferred type for a column are captured in the rescued data column instead of causing the record to be dropped",
        "optionD": "The rescued data column automatically repairs mistyped values by casting them to the declared type before writing",
        "optionE": "Records with rescued values are excluded from the target table and written to a separate quarantine table instead",
        "answer": [
            "optionA",
            "optionC"
        ],
        "type": "multi"
