        "question_no": "Q41",
        "question": "Stakeholders open a dashboard each morning and complain that it shows yesterday's figures until someone opens it and waits for the queries to finish running. Which configuration addresses this most directly?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "This question tests dashboard scheduling, and distinguishes treating a cause from treating a symptom.\nDiagnosing the Complaint\nTwo things are happening, and they share one root cause:\nStale figures \u2014 the dashboard shows cached results from the last time someone ran it.\nA wait on open \u2014 the first viewer each morning triggers the queries and waits.\nBoth follow from the dashboard only refreshing when a human opens it. Nothing has run since yesterday.\nWhat a Schedule Changes\nA scheduled refresh runs the underlying queries on a cadence against a SQL warehouse and caches the results. Set it to complete before the business day and the first viewer sees current figures immediately \u2014 no trigger, no wait.\nSequencing matters: schedule the refresh after the upstream pipeline finishes, or you'll cache yesterday's data very promptly. This is why dashboard refresh is often expressed as a dashboard task in the same job that builds the data, chained after the pipeline task \u2014 the dependency is then explicit rather than a guess about timing.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Increase the SQL warehouse size\" \u2014 treats the symptom. A larger warehouse shortens the wait but doesn't remove it, the stakeholder still triggers the work, and you pay more for every query permanently.\n\"Convert to a materialized view \u2014 views are always current\" \u2014 contains a real error worth correcting. A materialized view stores precomputed results and must be refreshed; it is not always current. The phrase \"always current\" describes a standard view, which recomputes on every read \u2014 reintroducing exactly the wait being complained about. Materialized views are genuinely useful here, but they need scheduling too.\n\"File arrival trigger on the dashboard\" \u2014 file arrival triggers apply to jobs, not dashboards. Dashboards refresh on a schedule or on demand.\nKey Takeaway\nAdd a schedule to the dashboard so its queries refresh on a cadence before stakeholders open it \u2014 sequenced after the upstream pipeline. Warehouse sizing treats the symptom; materialized views still require refresh.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Configure and edit Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/configure-job\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/\nDatabricks \u2014 Databricks SQL \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/",
        "optionA": "Convert the dashboard's queries into a materialized view, which removes the need for any refresh because views are always current",
