        "question_no": "Q45",
        "question": "A BI team asks for a Gold layer model in which sales measures can be sliced by product, store, and date. The engineer plans a central table holding the numeric measures and the keys, surrounded by smaller tables holding descriptive attributes. What is this design called, and what is its main benefit for BI?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests recognizing a star schema from its description and knowing why it dominates BI modelling.\nReading the Structure\nThe description names both components precisely:\nA central table holding numeric measures and keys \u2014 this is a fact table. Facts are the things you measure: sales amount, quantity, discount. It also holds foreign keys to its dimensions.\nSmaller surrounding tables holding descriptive attributes \u2014 these are dimension tables. Dimensions are the things you slice by: product name and category, store city and region, date's month and quarter.\nOne fact table at the center with dimensions radiating outward is a star, which is where the name comes from.\nWhy BI Tools Favor It\nEvery analytical question collapses into the same shape: aggregate a measure from the fact table, join one or more dimensions, group and filter on their attributes.\nThat predictability is what BI tools are built around. Because dimensions are small and the join pattern is uniform, the joins are cheap \u2014 typically broadcast \u2014 and the optimizer handles them well. It's also comprehensible to non-engineers, which matters when analysts build their own reports.\nWhere It Sits in the Medallion Architecture\nA star schema is a Gold layer pattern. Silver holds cleaned data close to source grain; Gold reshapes it for a specific audience. Denormalizing dimensions is the deliberate trade covered in Exam 1 Q2 \u2014 redundancy accepted in exchange for read performance and clarity.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nSnowflake schema \u2014 a real variant where dimensions are normalized into further sub-tables. But that's not what's described (the dimensions here are flat), and the stated benefit is backwards: extra normalization adds joins rather than speeding aggregation. That's precisely why star is generally preferred for BI.\nMedallion architecture \u2014 describes refinement stages (raw \u2192 cleaned \u2192 curated), not the relational shape of tables within a stage. The two are complementary: a star schema is one way to organize Gold.\nSlowly changing dimension \u2014 a technique for tracking history within a dimension, such as a customer changing address. It applies to descriptive attributes rather than measures, and describes one dimension's behavior rather than the overall model.\nKey Takeaway\nA star schema places a fact table of measures and keys at the center, surrounded by dimension tables of descriptive attributes \u2014 giving BI tools simple, predictable joins. It's a Gold layer pattern.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Medallion architecture \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/medallion\nDatabricks \u2014 Constraints on Databricks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/constraints\nDatabricks \u2014 Query optimization using constraints \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/user/queries/query-optimization-constraints",
        "optionA": "A medallion architecture - the sales table is Bronze and the descriptive tables are its Silver refinements",
        "optionB": "A slowly changing dimension - the surrounding tables track how the measures have changed over time",
        "optionC": "A snowflake schema - normalizing each dimension into further sub-tables is what makes the measures efficient to aggregate",
        "optionD": "A star schema - the fact table carries the measures and keys while the dimension tables carry descriptive attributes, so BI tools aggregate measures and filter on attributes through simple, predictable joins",
        "answer": [
            "optionD"
        ],
        "type": "single"
