        "question_no": "Q52",
        "question": "A team's bundle deploys a job that runs a notebook importing a shared Python module from the repository. Which TWO statements about how a bundle handles these files are correct? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionC, optionE",
        "explanation": "This question tests which files a bundle actually deploys \u2014 and the run-time failure that follows from getting it wrong.\nBundles Deploy Files, Not Just Definitions\nA bundle uploads the workspace files it is configured to include \u2014 notebooks, Python modules, SQL files \u2014 to the target workspace alongside the job and pipeline definitions.\nThat coupling is the guarantee: the deployed job runs the code version that shipped with it. Nobody can change the behavior of a deployed job by editing something in the workspace, because the next deploy restores what the configuration describes.\nThe Gotcha: Configuration Determines Scope\nThe bundle deploys what its configuration tells it to. A shared module living outside the configured paths is simply absent in the target workspace.\nThe failure mode is what makes this worth knowing. The deployment succeeds \u2014 nothing is missing from the bundle's perspective. The problem surfaces later, at run time, as an import error in a job that worked perfectly in development because the module was sitting in the developer's workspace all along.\nTwo symptoms make it recognizable: it works in dev and fails in test, and the error is an import failure rather than anything about deployment.\nThe Practical Habit\nAfter a first deployment to a new target, look at the deployed file tree in the workspace. Confirm the modules are there. Thirty seconds that saves a confusing debugging session.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Bundles deploy only resource definitions; sync notebooks via Git Folders\" \u2014 describes the manual workflow bundles replace, and it breaks the guarantee that a job runs the code version it was deployed with. A Git Folder can be on any branch at any moment.\n\"Any file in the repository is uploaded automatically\" \u2014 would sweep up tests, documentation, README files, local scratch notebooks, and CI configuration. Explicit path configuration is what keeps deployments deliberate and small.\n\"Python modules are installed as cluster libraries, so no path configuration is needed\" \u2014 packaging a module as a wheel and installing it as a library is a valid option, which makes this the strongest distractor. But it's one choice among several rather than automatic behavior, and plain modules are uploaded as workspace files.\nKey Takeaway\nA bundle uploads the workspace files its configuration includes, so a deployed job runs the code that shipped with it. Files outside those paths are not deployed, and the resulting import error appears at run time rather than at deploy time.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Declarative Automation Bundles configuration \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/settings\nDatabricks \u2014 Declarative Automation Bundles resources \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/resources\nDatabricks \u2014 Configuration reference \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/reference",
        "optionA": "Any file present in the repository is uploaded automatically, regardless of the bundle's configuration",
