        "question_no": "Q53",
        "question": "A logistics company must choose a processing model for vehicle telemetry. Dispatchers need to see a vehicle's position within seconds of it moving, while the finance team needs a mileage summary once per day. Which approach fits, and why?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests matching processing model to consumer requirement \u2014 and specifically resisting the urge to standardize on one.\nTwo Consumers, Two Requirements\nDispatchers need a vehicle's position within seconds. Latency has direct operational cost: a dispatcher routing against a two-minute-old position sends a driver the wrong way.\nFinance needs a mileage summary once per day. Latency has no cost whatsoever. A summary computed at 6 a.m. is as useful as one computed continuously.\nWhy the Answer Is Both\nStreaming buys low latency with continuous compute. That purchase is worth making for dispatchers and pointless for finance \u2014 paying 24 hours of readiness to serve one daily read.\nThe engineering instinct to standardize is understandable but wrong here. Two genuinely different requirements justify two models, and forcing either one onto the other means overpaying or under-serving.\nThe Architecture in Practice\nThese usually share infrastructure rather than duplicating it. Telemetry streams into a Bronze table continuously. The dispatcher view reads that stream with low latency. The finance summary runs as a scheduled batch job against the same Bronze table each morning.\nOne ingestion path, two consumption patterns, each sized to its requirement \u2014 which is precisely what the medallion architecture is for.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Batch for both \u2014 streaming cannot produce daily aggregates\" \u2014 the first clause is false: windowed and stateful aggregations are core Structured Streaming capabilities. The maintenance concern is fair, but it does not justify making dispatchers wait for a batch cycle when their requirement is seconds.\n\"Streaming for both \u2014 the batch summary comes free\" \u2014 the summary still consumes compute to produce, and keeping a stream alive to serve a once-daily read means paying for 24 hours of readiness. Nothing is free.\n\"Batch for dispatchers, streaming for finance\" \u2014 inverts the requirements entirely. The dispatcher view is the latency-sensitive one; a daily mileage total is not made urgent by being an aggregate.\nKey Takeaway\nMatch the processing model to each consumer's latency requirement. Streaming buys low latency with continuous compute \u2014 worth it where seconds matter, wasteful where they don't. Both models can share one ingestion path.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Configure Structured Streaming trigger intervals \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/triggers\nDatabricks \u2014 Medallion architecture \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/medallion\nDatabricks \u2014 Production considerations for Structured Streaming \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/production",
        "optionA": "Stream processing for both, because once a streaming pipeline exists the batch summary can be derived from it at no additional cost",
        "optionB": "Batch processing for the dispatcher view with a one-minute schedule, and stream processing for the finance summary, because aggregation is the more latency-sensitive operation",
        "optionC": "Batch processing for both, because streaming cannot produce daily aggregates and running two models doubles the maintenance burden",
        "optionD": "Stream processing for the dispatcher view and batch processing for the finance summary - streaming pays for continuous compute to buy low latency, which only the dispatcher use case needs",
        "answer": [
            "optionD"
        ],
        "type": "single"
