        "question_no": "Q55",
        "question": "In a medallion architecture, a team argues about what belongs in the Silver layer. One proposal is to place the finished per-region revenue summary there so that dashboards can read it directly. Which response best reflects the purpose of the Silver layer?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "This question tests what belongs in Silver versus Gold \u2014 and why the boundary is about reusability, not refinement level alone.\nSilver's Defining Property: General-Purpose\nSilver is where data becomes trustworthy but still general. Types are enforced, duplicates removed, sources conformed, nulls handled \u2014 but the grain stays close to the source. One row per order, one row per event, one row per customer.\nThat grain is the point. Because Silver preserves detail, many different consumers can build many different things from it: revenue by region, revenue by product, a churn feature table, an operational report. None of them needs a different Silver.\nWhy the Summary Belongs in Gold\nA per-region revenue summary is one specific answer to one specific question, aggregated to a grain that discards everything else. Putting it in Silver collapses the layer's flexibility: the next team needing revenue by product finds a table already aggregated by region, with the detail gone. They must go back to Silver-that-should-have-existed \u2014 or, in practice, back to Bronze.\nThe Test to Apply\nAsk: could several different consumers build different things from this?\nYes \u2192 Silver. It's general-purpose.\nNo, it answers one question for one audience \u2192 Gold. It's purpose-built.\nThe Three Layers in One Line Each\nBronze \u2014 raw as delivered; enables replay and audit.\nSilver \u2014 cleaned and conformed at source grain; enables many consumers.\nGold \u2014 aggregated and shaped for a specific audience; optimized for reading.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Silver holds finished aggregates; Gold holds raw data\" \u2014 inverts the architecture entirely. Raw source data is Bronze, and business aggregates are Gold.\n\"Silver holds whatever is queried most often\" \u2014 query frequency describes usage, not refinement. A heavily read Gold table is still Gold. Organizing layers by popularity would make the architecture meaningless within a quarter.\n\"Silver and Gold differ only in naming\" \u2014 the distinction is substantive: general-purpose and reusable versus purpose-built and aggregated. Treating them as interchangeable labels produces a lake where nobody can tell which table to trust.\nKey Takeaway\nSilver holds cleaned, conformed data at roughly source grain, so many consumers can build different aggregates from it. A single finished summary belongs in Gold.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Medallion architecture \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/medallion\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Delta Lake in Databricks? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta\nDatabricks \u2014 Streaming tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/streaming-tables",
        "optionA": "Silver and Gold differ only in naming convention, so the placement has no practical consequence",
        "optionB": "Silver holds cleaned, conformed, deduplicated records at roughly the grain of the source, so that many downstream consumers can build different aggregates from it; a single finished summary belongs in Gold",
        "optionC": "Silver holds finished business aggregates, so the revenue summary is placed correctly and Gold should hold the raw source data",
        "optionD": "Silver holds whatever is queried most often, so placing the summary there is correct if dashboards are the heaviest consumers",
