        "question_no": "Q56",
        "question": "A team needs a copy of a 40 TB production table for a week of testing. The copy will be read heavily and written to occasionally, and it must not affect production. Storage cost matters. Which approach fits, and what is the trade-off?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question tests shallow versus deep clone \u2014 and the dependency that makes shallow clones both powerful and occasionally dangerous.\nThe Two Clone Types\nShallow clone \u2014 copies only metadata. The new table's log references the source's existing data files. Creation is near-instant regardless of table size, and it consumes almost no additional storage.\nDeep clone \u2014 copies metadata and data. Fully independent of the source, at the cost of time and storage proportional to the table.\nWhy Shallow Fits This Scenario\n40 TB, one week, storage cost matters. A deep clone means copying 40 TB \u2014 hours of compute and a doubled storage bill for a temporary environment. A shallow clone appears in seconds and adds essentially nothing.\nCritically, shallow clones are writable. Writes to the clone produce new files belonging to the clone; the source's files are only ever read. Production is untouched, which satisfies the isolation requirement.\nThe Dependency to Understand\nBecause the clone points at the source's files, it depends on those files continuing to exist. If someone runs VACUUM on the source with a short retention, files the clone references can be deleted \u2014 leaving the clone unreadable.\nThat's the trade-off stated honestly. It's manageable \u2014 avoid aggressive VACUUM on a source with live clones, and keep clone lifetimes short, as this one-week test does \u2014 but it's a real constraint rather than a footnote.\nChoosing Between Them\nShallow \u2014 short-lived test and development copies, experiments, \"what if I ran this migration\" scenarios.\nDeep \u2014 durable copies, cross-region backups, or anywhere independence from the source's lifecycle matters.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Shallow physically copies all 40 TB but bypasses the query engine\" \u2014 describes a deep clone's behavior while naming shallow. Copying nothing is what makes shallow instant.\n\"Deep copies only metadata; shallow copies data\" \u2014 reverses the two.\n\"Deep is the only writable type; shallow is read-only\" \u2014 shallow clones are writable, which is precisely what makes them useful for testing.\n\"A view gives testers an isolated writable copy\" \u2014 a view stores query text, not data, and is read-only. It also offers no isolation from changes in the source.\nKey Takeaway\nA shallow clone copies metadata only and references the source's files \u2014 instant, cheap, and writable. Its dependency is real: aggressive VACUUM on the source can break it. A deep clone copies the data and is independent.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Unity Catalog managed tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/managed\nDatabricks \u2014 Remove unused data files with vacuum \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/operations/vacuum\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Delta Lake in Databricks? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta",
        "optionA": "A shallow clone, which copies only the metadata and points at the source's existing data files, so it is created almost instantly and consumes little storage - but it depends on the source's files remaining present, so aggressive VACUUM on the source can break it",
        "optionB": "A view over the production table, which gives testers an isolated copy they can write to without touching the source",
        "optionC": "A shallow clone, which physically copies all 40 TB but does so faster than CTAS because it bypasses the query engine",
        "optionD": "A deep clone, which copies only metadata and is therefore the low-storage option, while a shallow clone copies the data",
        "optionE": "A deep clone, which is the only clone type that permits writes to the copy, since a shallow clone is read-only",
        "answer": [
            "optionA"
        ],
        "type": "single"
