        "question_no": "Q58",
        "question": "A Lakeflow Job must run the same processing task once for each of a list of country codes that is not known until the job runs - it is produced by an upstream task. Which feature expresses this?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests the for-each task \u2014 dynamic fan-out where the width isn't known until the job runs.\nWhy the Fan-Out Must Be Dynamic\nThe country list is produced by an upstream task. Nobody knows at design time whether it holds 3 entries or 300, and it may differ between runs.\nThat rules out anything requiring the iterations to be enumerated in advance.\nWhat a For-Each Task Provides\nIt accepts a list produced upstream and runs its nested task once per element. Two consequences matter operationally:\nPer-iteration visibility. Each iteration appears individually in the run, with its own status and duration. When Brazil fails, you see Brazil failed \u2014 not \"the country task failed.\"\nPer-iteration recovery. A repair run re-executes only the failed iterations, leaving the successful ones alone. On a 40-country fan-out where one failed, that's the difference between rerunning 1 and rerunning 40.\nIterations can also run in parallel, with a configurable concurrency limit so a fan-out doesn't overwhelm a downstream system.\nThe Control Flow Family\nWorth learning together, since the guide names them collectively:\nFor-each task \u2014 iterate over a list, width determined at run time.\nIf/else condition task \u2014 branch on a value.\nRun-if conditions \u2014 relax the default that all dependencies must succeed.\nRetry policies \u2014 recover from transient failures.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nOne task per country, maintained manually \u2014 cannot work when the list is produced at run time, and would require editing the job definition whenever the list changed.\nNotebook looping internally \u2014 the closest wrong answer, because it works. But it collapses the fan-out into one opaque task: iterations run sequentially, per-country status is invisible in the UI, one bad country fails everything, and a retry reruns all of them. The stated premise is also false \u2014 a for-each task handles a run-time-determined count precisely.\nFile arrival trigger \u2014 starts a job when files appear in cloud storage. A job-level trigger with no relationship to iterating over a list computed by a task.\nKey Takeaway\nA for-each task runs its nested task once per element of a list produced upstream, with each iteration individually tracked, retryable, and optionally parallel. Looping inside a notebook forfeits all of that.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/\nDatabricks \u2014 Configure and edit Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/configure-job\nDatabricks \u2014 Notebook task for jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/tasks/notebook",
        "optionA": "A notebook that loops over the list internally, since the number of iterations cannot be known before the run starts",
