        "question_no": "Q60",
        "question": "Several pipelines repeat the same logic for normalizing a phone number into a canonical format. The team wants that logic defined once and callable from SQL across notebooks and dashboards. Which approach fits?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests SQL UDFs as governed, reusable objects \u2014 and why duplicated logic is a governance problem, not just an aesthetic one.\nWhy Duplication Is the Real Issue\nPhone normalization repeated across several pipelines will diverge. Someone fixes an edge case \u2014 an international prefix, an extension \u2014 in one pipeline. The others keep the old behavior.\nNow the same customer's number is formatted differently depending on which pipeline produced the row, joins on phone number silently fail to match, and nobody notices until a report undercounts. Tracing it back to four slightly different expressions is genuinely difficult.\nWhat a SQL UDF Provides\nsql\nCREATE FUNCTION main.utils.normalize_phone(p STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nRETURN regexp_replace(p, '[^0-9]', '');\nOne definition. Registered to a catalog and schema like a table. A fix propagates to every caller automatically.\nCallable from anywhere. Any notebook, job, dashboard, or SQL query, by its three-level name.\nGoverned. A function is a securable, so EXECUTE is granted to the principals who need it \u2014 and access is audited like any other object.\nDiscoverable. It appears in the catalog, so the next engineer finds it rather than writing a fifth version.\nSQL UDF vs Python UDF\nPrefer SQL where the logic can be expressed in SQL. A SQL UDF's expression stays inside the engine, so it can be optimized and, where applicable, accelerated by Photon. A Python UDF requires serializing rows out to a Python process and back, and is opaque to the optimizer.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Copy the expression and keep copies in sync through review\" \u2014 review catches what a reviewer notices. Four copies of an expression will diverge, and the divergence is invisible until results disagree.\n\"Implement as a Python UDF; SQL functions cannot be registered permanently\" \u2014 the premise is false. SQL functions register to Unity Catalog, and a Python UDF would also perform worse for this kind of string manipulation.\n\"A temporary view, since a view is the only reusable SQL construct\" \u2014 a view encapsulates a query over specific tables, not a parameterized expression applicable to any column. A temporary view is also session-scoped and unreachable from other notebooks.\nKey Takeaway\nA SQL UDF registered to a catalog and schema defines logic once, is callable by name from any query, and is governed with the EXECUTE privilege. Prefer SQL UDFs over Python UDFs where the logic can be expressed in SQL.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 SQL language reference \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/\nDatabricks \u2014 Manage privileges in Unity Catalog \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Unity Catalog? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/",
        "optionA": "Copy the expression into each pipeline and keep the copies in sync through code review",
