        "question_no": "Q61",
        "question": "A regulated pipeline must not publish any batch in which a record is missing its account_id. Partial loads are unacceptable - if the constraint is violated, the update should stop so the team can investigate. Which expectation configuration matches?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "This question tests choosing the strictest expectation action \u2014 and why a regulated pipeline needs it.\nThe Requirement Behind the Requirement\n\"Partial loads are unacceptable.\"\nThat phrase does the work. It rules out anything that publishes some records while discarding others, because a partial batch in a regulated context is arguably worse than no batch: downstream reports look complete, reconcile against nothing, and the gap is invisible until an audit.\nWhat expect_or_fail Does\nWhen a record violates the expectation, the update stops. No partial batch reaches the target. The pipeline reports failure, the event log records which expectation failed and how many records violated it, and a human investigates before anything is published.\nThat's the correct behavior when correctness outranks availability.\nA Practical Refinement\nTeams often start a new source with expect to measure how often a rule actually fires, then tighten to drop or fail once the baseline is understood. Going straight to expect_or_fail on an unfamiliar source can mean a pipeline that halts nightly on a condition nobody anticipated.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"expect_or_fail discards records and logs them\" \u2014 the decorator is right, the behavior is wrong. It halts; it does not discard and continue.\n\"expect_or_drop discards and completes with the remainder\" \u2014 accurate description, wrong requirement. This publishes a partial batch, which the scenario explicitly forbids.\n\"expect records the violation and allows the record through\" \u2014 accurate, and the most permissive option. Invalid records would reach the target, which is the opposite of what a regulated pipeline needs.\nKey Takeaway\nexpect_or_fail halts the update on violation, so no partial batch is published \u2014 the right choice when partial loads are unacceptable. expect warns and keeps; expect_or_drop discards and continues.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Manage data quality with pipeline expectations \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations\nDatabricks \u2014 What are Lakeflow pipelines? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/\nDatabricks \u2014 Best practices for Lakeflow pipelines \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/best-practices",
