        "question_no": "Q63",
        "question": "A team receives three feeds: a fixed-width CSV export from an ERP system, a stream of JSON events with varying optional fields, and a folder of scanned contract PDFs. How should each be classified, and what does that imply for how they are stored in Databricks?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests data classification \u2014 and, more usefully, what each classification implies for storage in Databricks.\nThe Three Categories\nStructured \u2014 a fixed, uniform schema known in advance. Every record has the same fields with the same types. The fixed-width CSV from the ERP system qualifies: an export from a relational system with a defined layout.\nSemi-structured \u2014 self-describing but variable. Fields carry their names with them, and records need not agree on which fields are present. JSON events with varying optional fields is the textbook case.\nUnstructured \u2014 no queryable field structure at all. Scanned PDFs are images of documents. A human reads them; a query engine cannot address anything inside them without OCR or extraction first.\nWhat Each Implies for Storage\nStructured \u2192 Delta table. Declare the schema, load it, done.\nSemi-structured \u2192 Delta table, with variability handled. This is where the interesting work is. Because JSON records disagree about which fields are present, ingestion must have an answer for change: schema inference to establish a baseline, schema evolution to adopt new fields, and a rescued data column to catch whatever still doesn't fit. The table is typed; the pipeline absorbs the variability.\nUnstructured \u2192 Unity Catalog volume. Volumes exist for exactly this: governed non-tabular data, addressed at /Volumes/<catalog>/<schema>/<volume>/<path> from any supported language.\nThe Real Insight\nSemi-structured data is where most ingestion effort goes. Structured sources are easy and unstructured sources are simply files. It is the middle category \u2014 data with some structure that keeps changing \u2014 where schema evolution and rescue columns earn their existence.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"All three as Delta tables; Unity Catalog governs only tabular objects\" \u2014 the classification is right but the conclusion is wrong. Volumes govern non-tabular data, and PDFs have no column layout to register.\n\"JSON is structured; PDFs are semi-structured\" \u2014 JSON's self-describing but variable shape is the definition of semi-structured. A scanned PDF has no extractable field structure without OCR; being human-readable does not make data semi-structured.\n\"All unstructured until loaded into Delta\" \u2014 classification describes the data's inherent shape, not where it currently sits. Loading a PDF into Delta would not give it queryable structure.\nKey Takeaway\nStructured \u2192 Delta table. Semi-structured \u2192 Delta table with schema evolution and a rescue column absorbing variability. Unstructured \u2192 Unity Catalog volume.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Unity Catalog? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Auto Loader? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader\nDatabricks \u2014 Medallion architecture \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/medallion",
        "optionA": "All three are unstructured until they are loaded into Delta, at which point they all become structured",
