        "question_no": "Q64",
        "question": "A team removes a job definition from their bundle configuration and redeploys to the dev target. They expect the job to disappear from the workspace. What happens, and why?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests what makes a bundle declarative rather than a deployment script: it reconciles state.\nDeclarative vs Imperative Deployment\nImperative \u2014 a script issuing commands: create this job, update that pipeline. It only knows what you told it to do now, so it can create and update but never remove. Delete a job from the script and nothing happens to the deployed one.\nDeclarative \u2014 the configuration describes the desired end state. The bundle tracks which resources it created, compares that to the configuration, and reconciles: creating what's new, updating what changed, and removing what's gone.\nWhy Removal Matters\nWithout it, workspaces silently accumulate. Every refactor leaves the old job behind. Six months later there are dozens of jobs nobody recognizes, and nobody will delete them because nobody can prove they're unused \u2014 one might still be scheduled, might still feed a report, might belong to a team that left.\nReconciliation means the workspace matches the configuration, so the repository answers \"what exists and what does it run?\" That's what makes a bundle a deployment mechanism rather than a convenient way to run API calls.\nThe Corollary Worth Stating\nThe same property explains why manual workspace edits don't survive. A schedule changed by hand during an incident is overwritten at the next deploy, because the configuration doesn't describe it. That's not a bug \u2014 it's reconciliation working. Anything meant to persist belongs in the repository.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"The job remains; a deploy only creates and updates\" \u2014 describes imperative script behavior, and is exactly the accumulation problem bundles solve.\n\"Renamed to indicate it is orphaned\" \u2014 no such behavior. A renamed job would still be schedulable and would still require someone to clean it up, so it would solve nothing.\n\"The deployment fails; removal requires destroying the target\" \u2014 removing a resource is an ordinary configuration change handled by a normal deploy. destroy exists for tearing down an entire target deliberately, not as a prerequisite for editing configuration.\nKey Takeaway\nA bundle tracks the resources it created, so a deploy reconciles the workspace to the configuration \u2014 including removing resources you deleted. This is what makes the repository the source of truth.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Declarative Automation Bundles resources \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/resources\nDatabricks \u2014 Declarative Automation Bundles configuration \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/settings\nDatabricks \u2014 bundle command group \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/bundle-commands",
