        "question_no": "Q65",
        "question": "A Delta table is partitioned by event_timestamp at day granularity and also by customer_id, which has 900,000 distinct values. Queries have become slow and the table's file count is enormous. Which TWO statements about this situation are correct? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionC, optionD",
        "explanation": "The Arithmetic\nHive-style partitioning creates a directory per distinct value combination. Here that's 900,000 customers \u00d7 every day in the table. Even one year produces hundreds of millions of potential partition directories.\nEach holds a tiny fraction of the data \u2014 often a single file of a few kilobytes. Spark then spends more time listing directories and opening files than reading data. This is the small-file problem in its purest form, and it's created by the layout rather than by the write pattern.\nWhy Partitioning Went Wrong Here\nPartitioning works when each partition is large enough to be worth reading as a unit. A day-granularity partition on a busy table might hold gigabytes \u2014 worth skipping wholesale when a query filters on date.\ncustomer_id is the opposite. High cardinality means each partition is minuscule, so the skipping benefit is swamped by the per-file overhead. The rule of thumb: partition on low-cardinality columns only.\nThe Fix\nRemove customer_id as a partition column and cluster or Z-order on it instead. Both co-locate related values within larger files, and Delta's per-file min/max statistics then skip the files that cannot match \u2014 delivering the customer-filter performance without fragmenting the table.\nLiquid clustering is the current recommendation, since keys can be changed later without rewriting the table. Note this requires rewriting the table once to change the partitioning, which is unavoidable \u2014 partition layout is physical.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"VACUUM merges small files\" \u2014 the most costly confusion in Delta maintenance. VACUUM deletes files unreferenced by the current version. It never merges live files \u2014 that's OPTIMIZE.\n\"Partitioning is required above 1 TB\" \u2014 no size threshold mandates partitioning, and current guidance favors liquid clustering over partitioning for most large tables.\n\"More partition columns always improve skipping\" \u2014 improvement continues only while partitions stay large enough to be worth reading as units. Past that point, additional partition columns actively hurt \u2014 which is exactly what has happened.\nKey Takeaway\nPartition on low-cardinality columns only. High-cardinality partitioning creates enormous numbers of tiny files where per-file overhead dominates. Use clustering or Z-order for skipping on high-cardinality columns \u2014 and VACUUM never merges files.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Use liquid clustering for tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/clustering\nDatabricks \u2014 Data skipping \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/data-skipping\nDatabricks \u2014 Optimize data file layout \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/operations/optimize",
        "optionA": "Increasing the number of partition columns always improves data skipping, so the file count is an acceptable cost",
        "optionB": "Running VACUUM will merge the small files into larger ones and resolve the file count problem",
