        "question_no": "Q66",
        "question": "A JSON ingestion job encounters occasional records that are not valid JSON. The team wants the malformed records preserved for investigation rather than silently discarded, and the batch must not fail. Which read mode fits?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question tests the three parse modes \u2014 how a reader responds to input it cannot parse.\nThe Three Modes\nPERMISSIVE (default) \u2014 sets unparseable fields to null and preserves the original record in a corrupt-record column. The read completes.\nDROPMALFORMED \u2014 silently discards records that cannot be parsed. The read completes with fewer rows.\nFAILFAST \u2014 raises an error on the first malformed record. The read stops.\nMatching the Requirements\nTwo requirements, and only one mode satisfies both:\n\"Preserved for investigation rather than silently discarded\" \u2192 rules out DROPMALFORMED.\n\"The batch must not fail\" \u2192 rules out FAILFAST.\nPERMISSIVE remains, and it does exactly what's asked: the record lands, parseable fields are populated, unparseable ones are null, and the raw text sits in the corrupt-record column ready to be queried.\nWhy PERMISSIVE Is the Right Default\nData loss is usually the worst outcome. A dropped record leaves no evidence \u2014 you cannot tell the source what they sent, cannot diagnose the pattern, and cannot reprocess once fixed. PERMISSIVE keeps the evidence.\nThe practical follow-up: query the corrupt-record column periodically. Records appearing there mean the source changed or something upstream broke, and the raw text tells you exactly what.\nWhen the Others Are Right\nFAILFAST \u2014 a regulated pipeline where malformed input must never pass silently. Failing loudly is the point.\nDROPMALFORMED \u2014 rarely the best choice, since it discards evidence. PERMISSIVE gives the same completion with the data retained.\nNote the parallel with Auto Loader's rescued data column, which applies the same philosophy to type mismatches and unknown fields.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"DROPMALFORMED retains records in a separate column while dropping them\" \u2014 self-contradictory. Dropping means nothing is retained.\n\"IGNORE, logging contents to the driver log\" \u2014 there is no IGNORE parse mode, and driver logs are an unreliable place to keep data you intend to investigate.\n\"PERMISSIVE repairs records by inferring the intended structure\" \u2014 the mode is right but the behavior is wrong. Nothing is repaired. Guessing at unparseable input would introduce silent errors, so the raw text is preserved verbatim.\n\"FAILFAST stops at the first malformed record\" \u2014 accurate, but violates the requirement that the batch complete.\nKey Takeaway\nPERMISSIVE nulls unparseable fields and preserves the raw record in a corrupt-record column, completing the read. DROPMALFORMED discards; FAILFAST errors. Query the corrupt-record column to catch source changes.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Auto Loader? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader\nDatabricks \u2014 Schema enforcement \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/schema-enforcement\nDatabricks \u2014 SQL language reference \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/",
