        "question_no": "Q67",
        "question": "A single sales table must be readable by regional managers, but each manager should see only their own region's rows. The team does not want to duplicate the table. Which approach implements this, and how does it determine the caller's region?",
        "Correct answer": "optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests row-level security \u2014 one object serving many audiences by evaluating the caller.\nThe Core Mechanism\nBoth valid approaches share one idea: the query evaluates who is asking.\nis_account_group_member('emea_managers') returns true or false depending on the current caller. Because it's evaluated per query, the same object returns different rows to different people.\nThe Two Implementations\nDynamic view \u2014 put the test in the view's predicate:\nsql\nCREATE VIEW sales_by_region AS\nSELECT * FROM sales\nWHERE (is_account_group_member('emea_managers') AND region = 'EMEA')\nOR (is_account_group_member('apac_managers') AND region = 'APAC');\nManagers query the view; access to the base table is not granted.\nRow filter \u2014 attach a filter function to the table itself. A row filter is a SQL UDF evaluated per row at query time, restricting which rows a user sees.\nWhy Row Filters Are Usually Better\nA view protects only those who use the view. Anyone with access to the base table bypasses it, so the base table's grants must be managed carefully alongside.\nA row filter attaches to the table, so it applies on every access path \u2014 SQL editor, notebooks, BI tools, jobs. There is nothing to bypass, because the filtering happens inside the engine regardless of how the table is reached.\nComposing With Column Masks\nRow filters restrict which rows; column masks change what a column returns. They're orthogonal and commonly used together \u2014 restrict rows by region and mask a sensitive column for non-authorized groups.\nAt scale, ABAC policies apply both centrally based on tags rather than table by table.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Column mask on the region column\" \u2014 masks change values, not row visibility. Masking region hides the label while still returning every region's rows \u2014 worse than useless, since managers would see other regions' data with the identifying column obscured.\n\"Grant SELECT and rely on managers to filter\" \u2014 a convention, not a control. Any manager could omit the filter, deliberately or by accident, and nothing would prevent or record it.\n\"One view per region, since a single view cannot vary by caller\" \u2014 the premise is false, and the approach multiplies objects: every new region means another view, another grant, and another thing to keep consistent.\nKey Takeaway\nUse a dynamic view testing is_account_group_member, or better a row filter attached to the table \u2014 both evaluate the caller at query time so one object serves many audiences. Row filters apply on every access path; views only protect those who use them.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Row filters and column masks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/\nDatabricks \u2014 Manually apply row filters and column masks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/manually-apply\nDatabricks \u2014 Attribute-based access control in Unity Catalog \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/",
