        "question_no": "Q83",
        "question": "A scheduled job has begun failing before any task runs. The run's compute page reports that the cluster could not be provisioned, citing an instance limit in the cloud region. What is the most likely cause, and what resolves it?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question tests cluster startup failures \u2014 and specifically reading the error to identify which layer failed.\nThe Decisive Clue: Nothing Ran\nThe job failed before any task started. That single fact eliminates every code-related explanation, because notebook code is not executed \u2014 or even parsed \u2014 until a cluster exists and a task begins.\nSo the failure is in provisioning: the layer where Databricks requests instances from the cloud provider.\nWhat an Instance Limit Means\nCloud accounts carry quotas \u2014 a maximum number of instances, vCPUs, or instances of a particular family, per region. These are provider-side limits, not Databricks limits.\nWhen a job asks for more than remains available, the provider refuses and the cluster cannot start. The error names the limit, which is what makes it identifiable.\nThe Three Remedies\nRaise the quota \u2014 request an increase from the cloud provider. Correct when the demand is legitimate, but it takes time, so it's the wrong thing to discover at 2 a.m.\nChoose a different node type \u2014 a different instance family likely has headroom. Fastest fix.\nReduce the worker count \u2014 ask for fewer instances. Slower job, but it runs.\nWhy It Started Failing Now\nQuota errors usually appear because something else changed \u2014 another team's workload grew, a new pipeline launched, or a cluster that should have terminated didn't. Checking what else is consuming the quota often matters more than fixing the immediate job.\nThe Diagnostic Pattern\nNothing ran \u2192 provisioning: quotas, permissions, network, capacity.\nCluster started, task failed immediately \u2192 libraries or configuration.\nTask ran then failed \u2192 application code or data.\nPlacing the failure on this ladder before investigating saves the most time.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nNotebook syntax error \u2014 code isn't parsed during provisioning. A syntax error surfaces when the task executes, which is after the cluster is running. Here nothing ran.\nLibrary installation failure \u2014 libraries install after the cluster is up, so this appears as a library error on a running cluster, not a provisioning failure citing an instance limit.\nRuntime deprecated overnight \u2014 runtimes aren't withdrawn without notice, and deprecation produces an explicit message about the runtime version rather than a cloud instance limit.\nKey Takeaway\nA cluster that cannot provision citing an instance limit has hit a cloud provider quota. Raise the quota, choose a different node type, or reduce workers. If no task ran, the cause is in provisioning \u2014 not in code.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Compute configuration reference \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/configure\nDatabricks \u2014 Compute \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/",
        "optionA": "The cloud account has hit its instance quota for that node type in that region; raising the quota, choosing a different node type, or reducing the worker count resolves it",
        "optionB": "The Databricks Runtime version was deprecated overnight, which manifests as a provisioning error; selecting a current runtime resolves it",
        "optionC": "The cluster's libraries failed to install, which prevents provisioning; removing the libraries resolves it",
        "optionD": "The job's notebook contains a syntax error, which is detected during cluster provisioning; fixing the code resolves it",
        "answer": [
            "optionA"
        ],
        "type": "single"
