        "question_no": "Q84",
        "question": "A COPY INTO load of CSV files fails because the source added a column that the target Delta table does not have. Which change allows the load to succeed while adding the new column to the table?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "This question tests COPY INTO's copy options \u2014 and specifically that batch ingestion handles schema evolution too.\nWhy the Load Failed\nDelta validates schema on write. The incoming CSV carries a column the target doesn't have, so the write is rejected rather than silently guessing what to do with it.\nSame enforcement that protects a DataFrame append, applied to COPY INTO.\nThe Two Option Families\nCOPY INTO takes two distinct kinds of option, and knowing which is which matters:\nFORMAT_OPTIONS \u2014 how to read the source files. For CSV: header, delimiter, inferSchema, nullValue, and so on. These describe the input.\nCOPY_OPTIONS \u2014 how COPY INTO behaves. This is where mergeSchema lives, alongside force. These describe the operation.\nSchema merging is a property of how the load treats the target, so it belongs in COPY_OPTIONS.\nThe Result\nWith schema merging enabled, the new column is added to the target table and existing rows carry null for it. Existing data is untouched \u2014 only the schema is extended. Identical semantics to mergeSchema on a DataFrame write.\nThe Broader Point\nSchema evolution is often assumed to be a streaming-only concern, because Auto Loader's evolution modes get the attention. It isn't. COPY INTO handles additive source changes too, which matters when choosing between the two: schema drift alone doesn't force you into a streaming path.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Switch to Auto Loader \u2014 only streaming handles schema changes\" \u2014 the premise is false. Both paths handle additive changes. The choice between them should turn on file volume, arrival pattern, and execution model, not this.\n\"Recreate the target table\" \u2014 the premise is false, and it would discard both the existing rows and the table's version history to accommodate one new column.\n\"A format option that ignores unknown columns\" \u2014 this drops the column, losing data with no signal, and the team's stated goal is to add it. Wrong family of option and wrong outcome.\nKey Takeaway\nSupply the schema-merging copy option so COPY INTO adds the new column to the target as part of the load. FORMAT_OPTIONS describe how to read files; COPY_OPTIONS describe how the load behaves.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Get started using COPY INTO to load data \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/copy-into\nDatabricks \u2014 Update table schemas with schema evolution \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/update-schema\nDatabricks \u2014 Schema enforcement \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/schema-enforcement",
        "optionA": "Supply a format option that ignores unknown columns, which drops the new column and completes the load",
        "optionB": "Supply the copy option that merges the schema, so COPY INTO adds the new column to the target table as part of the load",
        "optionC": "Recreate the target table from the new file, since COPY INTO cannot alter a target table's schema",
        "optionD": "Switch to Auto Loader, since schema changes can only be handled by a streaming ingestion path",
        "answer": [
            "optionB"
        ],
        "type": "single"
