        "question_no": "Q158",
        "question": "A team must let an on-call engineer start and cancel a job's runs during an incident, without allowing them to change what the job does. Which TWO statements about job permissions are correct? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionA, optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests job permission levels \u2014 and how granularity enables a common operational split.\nOperating a Job vs Changing It\nThese are different capabilities, and job permissions separate them:\nView \u2014 see the job, its configuration, and its run history. Read-only.\nManage runs \u2014 trigger a run and cancel one. Operate the job.\nManage \u2014 modify the definition: tasks, schedule, compute, notifications.\nOwner \u2014 full control including deletion and permission management.\nWhy This Split Matters at 3 a.m.\nThe scenario is the reason granularity exists.\nAn on-call engineer needs to rerun a failed job or cancel a runaway one. That is operating it.\nThey should not be changing what the job does. Modifying a production job's logic during an incident, under pressure, without review, is how a small outage becomes a large one.\nGranting manage runs without manage gives exactly the capability the situation calls for and nothing more.\nWhere This Fits\nThe same pattern appears throughout: SELECT without MODIFY, USE CATALOG without CREATE TABLE, browse without read. Each privilege names one capability so a role can be described precisely.\nFor jobs specifically, this typically means: the owning team holds manage, on-call holds manage runs, and stakeholders hold view.\nA Related Distinction\nJob permissions govern who may operate the job. The run-as identity governs what the job may access. Separate concerns \u2014 an on-call engineer with manage-runs triggers a job that still executes with the service principal's data privileges, not their own.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Inherited from Unity Catalog privileges on the tables the job touches\" \u2014 jobs are workspace objects governed by their own access control lists. Unity Catalog governs the data. Two systems, two object types \u2014 a distinction the exam returns to repeatedly.\n\"Viewing implies the ability to trigger\" \u2014 separate levels. Conflating them would mean anyone who could see a job could also launch expensive or destructive work.\n\"Only the owner can cancel a run\" \u2014 cancelling requires the run-management permission, grantable without ownership. Making an on-call engineer owner would hand them full control of the definition, which is what the requirement rules out.\nKey Takeaway\nJob permissions are granular: view, manage runs, manage, and owner. Granting manage runs without manage lets an on-call engineer trigger and cancel runs while the definition stays unchangeable by them.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/\nDatabricks \u2014 Configure and edit Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/configure-job\nDatabricks \u2014 Databricks administration overview \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/admin-concepts",
        "optionA": "Job permissions are granular, distinguishing the ability to view a job, to trigger and cancel its runs, and to modify its definition",
        "optionB": "Only the job's owner can cancel a run, so the on-call engineer must be made owner",
        "optionC": "Any user who can view a job can also trigger it, since viewing implies the ability to run",
        "optionD": "Granting the right to manage runs without the right to manage the job lets the on-call engineer trigger and cancel runs while the definition stays unchanged",
        "optionE": "Job permissions are inherited from Unity Catalog privileges on the tables the job reads and writes",
