        "question_no": "Q160",
        "question": "A cluster is configured to autoscale between 2 and 12 workers. Which TWO statements about this configuration are correct? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionA, optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests autoscaling \u2014 what the range means and what it buys.\nThe Range Is a Floor and a Ceiling\nConfiguring 2 to 12 workers declares two things:\nMinimum 2 \u2014 the cluster starts here and never drops below it. A floor guarantees baseline capacity so the first task doesn't wait for provisioning.\nMaximum 12 \u2014 the ceiling. Databricks will not exceed it regardless of demand, which bounds the cost of an unexpectedly heavy workload.\nBetween them, the cluster adds workers when pending work justifies it and releases them when it doesn't.\nWhy Both Directions Matter\nScaling up is the obvious half: a heavy stage gets more parallelism without anyone provisioning for the worst case in advance.\nScaling down is what makes it economically worthwhile. A cluster that grew to 12 for a demanding stage and stayed there would cost peak price all day. Releasing workers means cost tracks demand rather than the peak \u2014 which is the whole argument for autoscaling.\nSetting the Range Sensibly\nMinimum too low \u2014 the cluster scales up on every job, adding latency while workers provision.\nMinimum too high \u2014 you pay for idle capacity during quiet periods.\nMaximum too low \u2014 heavy stages are throttled.\nMaximum too high \u2014 a runaway query can consume far more than intended.\nThe range is a genuine tuning decision informed by observed workload, not a default to accept.\nAutoscaling and Auto-Termination Are Different\nThis is the distinction the question's distractors probe. Autoscaling adjusts the number of workers on a running cluster. Auto-termination stops the cluster entirely after an idle period.\nA cluster scaled down to its minimum is still running and still billed. Both settings are needed: autoscaling for elastic sizing during work, auto-termination to stop paying when there is none.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Adjusts the size of each worker\" \u2014 autoscaling changes the count, not the instance type. Instance type is fixed at configuration and does not vary during a cluster's life.\n\"Always runs at 12; the minimum applies after idle\" \u2014 running at the maximum continuously means paying peak cost at all times, which is exactly what autoscaling avoids.\n\"Removes the need for auto-termination, since the minimum incurs no charge\" \u2014 the load-bearing error. A cluster at its minimum is running and billed. Auto-termination remains necessary.\nKey Takeaway\nAutoscaling adjusts the number of workers between a floor and a ceiling, adding when work demands and releasing when it doesn't, so cost tracks demand rather than peak. A cluster at its minimum is still running \u2014 auto-termination is still required.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Compute configuration reference \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/configure\nDatabricks \u2014 Compute \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/\nDatabricks \u2014 Compute selection recommendations \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/choose-compute",
