        "question_no": "Q168",
        "question": "During a code review, an engineer proposes replacing TRUNCATE TABLE staging_orders with DROP TABLE staging_orders at the start of a reload script, arguing the two are equivalent. Which statement correctly contrasts them?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "Option A: This reverses them, which is a costly mistake to make in production.\n\nOption B: They differ in exactly the properties the requirement names, so the choice is a real one.\n\nOption C: TRUNCATE is a logged Delta operation and appears in DESCRIBE HISTORY like any other. DELETE FROM with no predicate achieves a similar result but generally does more work.\n\nOption D: The distinction is whether the object survives. TRUNCATE removes the rows and commits that as a new version, so the schema, grants, table properties, and history all persist - and time travel can still reach the previous contents. DROP removes the table itself, along with its permissions and history, and for a managed table it deletes the underlying data as well.",
