        "question_no": "Q183",
        "question": "An engineer edits a bundle-deployed job's schedule directly in the prod workspace UI to work around an incident. The change is not made in the repository. What happens at the next bundle deployment, and what is the implication?",
        "Correct answer": "optionA",
        "explanation": "This question tests the hotfix workflow \u2014 deploying an urgent fix without shipping unreleased work alongside it.\nThe Constraint\nmain contains the fix's natural home and several unrelated features not ready to release. Deploying main means deploying all of it.\nThat is the trap: under incident pressure, \"just deploy main\" is the tempting move, and it converts a one-line fix into a release of untested features. If something then breaks, you cannot tell which change caused it.\nThe Correct Sequence\n1. Branch from the commit currently deployed to prod. Not from main \u2014 from the exact commit prod is running. That commit is known-good in production.\n2. Apply only the fix. One change, minimal surface.\n3. Deploy that branch to prod. The deployment contains the known-good state plus one intentional change. If it fixes the problem, you know what fixed it. If it doesn't, you know the fix wasn't the answer \u2014 no ambiguity from twelve other changes.\n4. Merge the same fix into main. The step people forget under pressure, and the one that prevents recurrence. Without it, the next ordinary release deploys main \u2014 which lacks the fix \u2014 and silently reintroduces the defect. That failure is especially confusing, because the bug returns after being demonstrably fixed.\nWhy This Matters Beyond Process Tidiness\nMinimal blast radius is the point. An incident is exactly when you want the smallest possible change, because your ability to reason carefully is at its lowest and the cost of a second failure is at its highest.\nTagging production releases makes step 1 trivial \u2014 branch from the tag rather than hunting for the deployed commit in history.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Deploy main \u2014 it contains the fix\" \u2014 ships several untested features under cover of an urgent fix. If prod then breaks, the cause is ambiguous among many changes, and the incident escalates rather than resolving.\n\"Revert every unreleased feature, deploy, reapply afterward\" \u2014 churns main heavily, risks mistakes in both the reverting and the reapplying, and touches far more than branching from the deployed commit.\n\"Edit the job directly in prod, add to main later\" \u2014 the workspace now disagrees with the configuration that produced it, and the next deploy overwrites the fix \u2014 reintroducing the defect with no warning. \"Add to main later\" is a promise that competes with the next urgent thing.\nKey Takeaway\nBranch from the commit currently deployed to prod, apply only the fix, deploy that branch, and merge the same fix into main so the next release doesn't silently revert it. Minimal change, unambiguous outcome.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 CI/CD on Databricks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/ci-cd-bundles\nDatabricks \u2014 Databricks Git folders \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/repos/\nDatabricks \u2014 bundle command group \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/cli/bundle-commands",
        "optionA": "The deployment reconciles the job to the configuration, so the manual schedule change is overwritten - meaning the workaround silently disappears unless it is also made in the repository",
