        "question_no": "Q184",
        "question": "A nightly job reports success every morning, but analysts notice the target table has not changed for three days. No task failed and no alert fired. What is the most useful next step?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests the difference between execution success and data correctness \u2014 the blind spot in every failure-based alerting setup.\nWhy Nothing Alerted\nThe job ran. Every task completed without raising. The run is marked Succeeded, correctly.\nJob alerting monitors execution: did the code run, did it fail, did it take too long. A task that read zero rows and wrote zero rows did all of that flawlessly. There is no execution-level signal to fire on, and there never will be.\nThree days of nothing happening, reported as three days of success.\nWhat to Check\nRun output metrics \u2014 how many rows were read and written. Zero rows written confirms the job did nothing, immediately separating \"the job is broken\" from \"the job had nothing to do.\"\nDESCRIBE HISTORY on the target \u2014 shows whether any commits landed. No new versions confirms it from the table's side.\nTogether they take about two minutes and point the investigation upstream.\nThe Likely Causes\nZero rows processed almost always means the input disappeared, not that the job broke:\nThe source stopped delivering \u2014 an upstream job failed, a partner's feed lapsed\nA path changed \u2014 a date-partitioned prefix rolled over, or a bucket was reorganized\nA filter now excludes everything \u2014 a status value changed upstream, or a date comparison drifted\nAn incremental watermark advanced past the available data\nEach is an upstream problem that the job faithfully reported as success.\nThe Preventive Measure\nThis is why data-level monitoring exists alongside job alerting. A SQL alert on row count, freshness, or a business metric catches what execution monitoring structurally cannot:\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM gold.daily_summary\nWHERE load_date = current_date()\n-- alert if 0\nPipeline expectations serve the same purpose inside a declarative pipeline, recording per-rule counts that make a sudden drop visible.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Restart the cluster \u2014 a stale cluster discards writes\" \u2014 Delta writes commit atomically and either succeed or raise. Silently discarded writes are not a failure mode a restart addresses.\n\"Conclude the table is correct\" \u2014 the assumption that let three days pass. Execution success and data correctness are separate properties; confirming one says nothing about the other.\n\"Increase the retry count\" \u2014 retries repeat failures, and nothing failed. Retrying a run that correctly processes zero rows produces zero rows again.\nKey Takeaway\nA job that reads zero rows completes successfully \u2014 execution monitoring cannot detect it. Check run output metrics and DESCRIBE HISTORY, and add data-level monitoring for row counts or freshness alongside job alerts.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Work with table history \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/history\nDatabricks \u2014 Configure and edit Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/configure-job\nDatabricks \u2014 Manage data quality with pipeline expectations \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations",
        "optionA": "Increase the job's retry count, since a silent failure indicates the task did not complete properly",
        "optionB": "Restart the job cluster, since a stale cluster can cause writes to be discarded without error",
        "optionC": "Conclude that the table is correct, since a successful run and the absence of alerts together confirm the data was written",
        "optionD": "Inspect the run's output metrics and the table's history - a task that read zero source rows and wrote zero rows completes successfully, so the failure is upstream in the data rather than in the job's execution",
        "answer": [
            "optionD"
        ],
