        "question_no": "Q185",
        "question": "A team stores a database password in a Databricks secret scope so that a job can read it. Which TWO statements about this are correct? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionA, optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests secret scopes \u2014 the access control around them and the redaction that protects against accident.\nScopes Are Governed Objects\nA secret scope has its own permissions. Referencing a scope in code does not grant the right to read it \u2014 a principal must be granted read access on the scope explicitly.\nBy default the creator receives MANAGE, allowing them to read secrets in the scope, write to it, and manage its permissions. Everyone else, including a job's service principal, needs an explicit grant.\nThat separation matters. Code referencing a scope can be shared, reviewed, and committed freely, because possessing the reference is not possessing the secret.\nRedaction Is the Safety Net\nValues retrieved through the secrets utility are redacted in notebook output and logs.\nThis protects against the most common accident, which is not malice but carelessness: printing a variable while debugging, logging a connection string, dumping a config dictionary. Without redaction, one print() puts the credential into notebook output and job logs where it persists \u2014 readable by anyone with access to the run, indefinitely.\nRedaction means the platform catches the mistake. Worth understanding as a backstop rather than a licence: deliberately reconstructing a secret character by character can defeat it, so the control is against accident, not against a determined actor.\nHow the Pieces Fit\npython\npassword = dbutils.secrets.get(scope=\"prod-db\", key=\"password\")\nRepository holds the scope and key names. The value lives in the encrypted store. The job's service principal holds read access on the scope. Rotation updates the secret with no code change. Output is redacted.\nFour properties, and each addresses a distinct failure mode.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Any workspace user can read any scope\" \u2014 would make scopes pointless as a security control. Unrestricted access is exactly the situation they exist to prevent.\n\"Secrets are substituted into notebook source at deployment\" \u2014 nothing is substituted into source. The value is fetched at run time from the secret store, which is precisely why it never appears in the repository. If it were baked into source, keeping the repository private would be the only protection \u2014 and that's a much weaker position.\n\"The value can be read back in plain text from the UI\" \u2014 secrets are write-only by design. Verification is done by using the secret, not by displaying it, so no path exists for a value to be shown. A readable secret store would undermine the whole mechanism.\nKey Takeaway\nSecret scopes have their own permissions \u2014 referencing a scope is not the same as being permitted to read it. Values retrieved through the secrets utility are redacted in output and logs, and secrets are write-only: verify by using, never by reading back.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Secret management \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/security/secrets/\nDatabricks \u2014 Tutorial: Create and use a Databricks secret \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/security/secrets/example-secret-workflow\nDatabricks \u2014 Manage privileges in Unity Catalog \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges",
        "optionA": "Access to a secret scope is controlled by its own permissions, so a principal must be granted read access on the scope in addition to having access to the code that references it",
