        "question_no": "Q186",
        "question": "A team is documenting the processing guarantees of their Auto Loader pipeline. Which TWO statements are correct? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionC, optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests exactly-once processing \u2014 which requires two mechanisms, and neither alone is sufficient.\nThe Two Halves\nThe checkpoint answers which files have I consumed? Durable, external to the cluster, updated only when a batch commits successfully.\nThe Delta sink's atomic commit ensures a batch's rows all become visible or none do. There is no partially applied batch.\nTogether they give the end-to-end guarantee. Separately, neither does.\nTracing a Failure\nThis is where the guarantee is actually tested, and the mechanism becomes clear:\nBatch reads files A, B, C\nRows are computed\nCluster dies before the commit\nWhat survives? Nothing visible. The commit never landed, so no rows appear in the table \u2014 readers still see the previous version. And the checkpoint never advanced, so A, B, and C are still marked unprocessed.\nOn restart, the stream reads A, B, C again and commits. Result: exactly once. Not twice, because the first attempt left nothing behind. Not zero times, because the checkpoint didn't falsely advance.\nBoth properties are load-bearing. Remove either and the failure produces a wrong outcome.\nWhy the Sink Matters\nIf the sink allowed partial writes \u2014 as a plain file write does \u2014 the failed batch would leave some rows visible. Reprocessing would then duplicate them, and the checkpoint would be blameless. That is why the guarantee is stated as checkpoint plus atomic sink rather than checkpoint alone.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Guaranteed regardless of the sink\" \u2014 a sink without atomic commits can expose partially written data, so reprocessing after a failure duplicates it. The sink is half the guarantee.\n\"Only in file notification mode\" \u2014 both discovery modes identify files reliably. They differ in how new files are found \u2014 enumeration versus cloud events \u2014 not in the processing guarantee that follows.\n\"Requires a unique key so duplicates are rejected on insert\" \u2014 no key or deduplication step is involved. And Unity Catalog key declarations are informational and unenforced, so they could not reject a duplicate even if the design relied on them.\nKey Takeaway\nExactly-once comes from the checkpoint recording consumed files plus the Delta sink's atomic commit. A batch failing before commit leaves no visible rows and no checkpoint advance, so reprocessing produces the correct result once.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Auto Loader? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader\nDatabricks \u2014 Delta Lake table streaming reads and writes \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/delta-lake\nDatabricks \u2014 ACID guarantees on Databricks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid",
        "optionA": "Exactly-once is guaranteed regardless of the sink, since the checkpoint alone prevents any duplicate write",
        "optionB": "Exactly-once applies only in file notification mode, since directory listing cannot reliably identify a file",
