        "question_no": "Q189",
        "question": "An analyst cannot tell whether a column named amt in a shared table is gross or net, and there is no documentation. Which Databricks capability addresses this directly, and where does the result appear?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "This question tests comments as metadata \u2014 documentation that lives with the object rather than beside it.\nThe Problem Being Solved\nA column called amt could mean gross, net, pre-discount, post-tax, or something else. Getting it wrong produces a report that looks right and is wrong \u2014 the worst kind of error, because nothing surfaces it.\nRight now the only way to find out is to ask someone, and that person may have left.\nHow Comments Work\nComments attach to tables and to individual columns, at creation or afterward:\nsql\nCOMMENT ON TABLE sales.orders IS\n'One row per completed order. Excludes cancellations.';\nALTER TABLE sales.orders ALTER COLUMN amt\nCOMMENT 'Gross amount in GBP, before discount and tax.';\nWhy Attaching to the Object Matters\nBecause the comment is metadata on the object, it appears wherever the object does:\nCatalog Explorer \u2014 visible to someone browsing, at the moment they're deciding whether to use the column.\nDESCRIBE output \u2014 visible to someone in a notebook, at the moment they're writing the query.\nDiscovery and search \u2014 the description travels with the object.\nThat timing is the whole value. Documentation in a wiki requires knowing it exists and going to look; a comment appears unprompted, exactly where the question arises.\nComments Alongside Tags\nComments are free text for humans \u2014 nuance, caveats, definitions. Tags are structured metadata that policies and queries can act on \u2014 a PII tag driving an ABAC masking policy, for instance.\nDifferent purposes, commonly used together: comments explain, tags classify.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"A separate documentation table, the only supported way\" \u2014 the premise is false, and external documentation drifts from what it describes. Nobody updates it, and nobody thinks to check it.\n\"Rename to gross_amount, since Delta stores no descriptive metadata\" \u2014 a clearer name genuinely helps, but the premise is false and renaming is disruptive to every downstream consumer. A comment adds meaning without breaking anything. (Column mapping does make renames metadata-only, but the consumer breakage remains.)\n\"Notebook markdown cells, which Catalog Explorer indexes\" \u2014 markdown documents the analysis it sits in. It is not indexed against catalog objects and is invisible to someone browsing the table.\nKey Takeaway\nAdd comments to tables and individual columns. Stored as metadata on the object, they surface in Catalog Explorer and DESCRIBE output \u2014 where the question actually arises. Tags classify; comments explain.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Enrich tables with custom metadata \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/operations/custom-metadata\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Unity Catalog? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/\nDatabricks \u2014 Rename and drop columns with Delta Lake column mapping \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/features/column-mapping",
        "optionA": "A separate documentation table maintained by the data owners, which is the only supported way to record column meaning",
        "optionB": "Comments added to the table and to individual columns, which are stored as metadata and surfaced in Catalog Explorer and in DESCRIBE output",
        "optionC": "Notebook markdown cells describing the column, which Catalog Explorer indexes and displays alongside the table",
        "optionD": "Renaming the column to gross_amount, which is the only mechanism available because Delta stores no descriptive metadata",
        "answer": [
            "optionB"
        ],
        "type": "single"
