        "question_no": "Q190",
        "question": "A pipeline's datasets must be published into catalog prod and schema sales, so that analysts can query them by their three-level names. Where is this specified?",
        "Correct answer": "optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests where a pipeline's publishing destination is configured \u2014 and why it belongs to the pipeline rather than to its datasets.\nConfiguration, Not Code\nThe target catalog and schema are pipeline settings. The dataset definitions declare what each dataset is; the pipeline declares where its datasets are published.\nThat separation is deliberate, and it is what makes a pipeline promotable.\nWhy It Enables Promotion\nBecause the destination lives in configuration, the same dataset definitions deploy unchanged to every environment:\nDev target \u2192 catalog dev_analytics, schema sales\nTest target \u2192 catalog test_analytics, schema sales\nProd target \u2192 catalog prod_analytics, schema sales\nOnly the setting changes, typically supplied by a bundle target override alongside cluster sizing and notification recipients.\nThat gives the promotion guarantee: what ran in test is exactly what runs in prod, differing only in environment context. Had each dataset hard-coded its destination, moving between environments would mean editing every definition \u2014 and the first missed one writes dev output into production.\nThe Result for Analysts\nOnce published, datasets are ordinary Unity Catalog objects at prod.sales.<dataset>. Analysts query them by three-level name, grants work normally, lineage is captured, and Catalog Explorer shows them like anything else.\nThe pipeline's internals are invisible to consumers, which is the intent.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Nowhere \u2014 always the workspace default catalog\" \u2014 the destination is configurable, which is precisely what allows separate dev, test, and prod catalogs. A fixed default would make environment separation impossible.\n\"A USE CATALOG statement in the notebook's first cell\" \u2014 USE CATALOG sets a session's current catalog for ad-hoc queries. Pipeline publishing is resolved from configuration, not from a statement inside the source \u2014 and putting it in code would defeat per-target overriding.\n\"In each dataset's definition, declaring its own destination\" \u2014 hard-coding a destination per dataset breaks promotion entirely: every dataset would need editing to move environments, and the risk of missing one is exactly the failure this design prevents.\nKey Takeaway\nThe target catalog and schema are pipeline settings, not part of dataset definitions \u2014 commonly overridden per environment through bundle targets, so the same definitions promote unchanged from dev to prod.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Use Unity Catalog with pipelines \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/unity-catalog\nDatabricks \u2014 What are Lakeflow pipelines? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/\nDatabricks \u2014 Declarative Automation Bundles configuration \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/settings",
        "optionA": "In each dataset's definition, since every dataset must declare its own fully qualified destination independently",
        "optionB": "In the notebook's first cell with a USE CATALOG statement, which the pipeline honors for all of its datasets",
        "optionC": "In the pipeline's settings, which specify the target catalog and schema that its datasets are published to - and which are commonly overridden per environment through bundle targets",
        "optionD": "Nowhere - pipeline datasets are always published to the workspace default catalog and cannot be redirected",
        "answer": [
            "optionC"
        ],
