        "question_no": "Q196",
        "question": "An engineer creating a Git Folder is prompted for credentials for their Git provider. Which statement about this is correct?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests Git credentials in Databricks \u2014 and why they are deliberately per-user rather than shared.\nWhat the Credential Is For\nDatabricks Git folders are a Git client inside the workspace. Like any Git client, they must authenticate outbound to the Git provider \u2014 GitHub, GitLab, Azure DevOps, Bitbucket \u2014 to clone, pull, and push.\nThe engineer is already authenticated to Databricks by being logged in. This credential is for the other end of the connection.\nWhy Per-User\nCorrect commit attribution. Commits carry the engineer's own provider identity, so git blame and history show who actually wrote each change. With a shared credential every commit would appear to come from one account, destroying authorship history.\nPermissions reflect the individual. What each engineer can read, push, or merge follows from their repository permissions. Someone with read-only access to a protected repository cannot push to it, and the provider enforces that per identity.\nBranch protection works. Rules like \"require an approved pull request\" and \"the author cannot approve their own PR\" depend on knowing who the author is. A shared identity would make those rules meaningless \u2014 everyone would be the same person.\nCredential Types\nProviders are typically linked with OAuth or a personal access token. OAuth is preferable where available: short-lived, refreshable, and revocable without a shared secret circulating.\nFor CI, the equivalent is a service principal with its own credentials \u2014 the same principle, applied to automation. Human identities for humans, machine identities for machines.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"No credential needed for private repositories; Unity Catalog determines access\" \u2014 Unity Catalog governs data and AI assets inside Databricks: catalogs, schemas, tables, volumes, functions. It has no relationship with a Git provider's repository permissions, which are an entirely separate system.\n\"A single workspace-wide credential for all users\" \u2014 would attribute every commit to one identity, destroying authorship history and making branch protection rules unenforceable.\n\"The credential grants access to the Databricks workspace\" \u2014 inverts the direction. The engineer is already authenticated to the workspace; this credential authenticates outward to the Git provider.\nKey Takeaway\nGit credentials are stored per user so commits carry each engineer's own provider identity and access reflects their own repository permissions \u2014 which is what makes branch protection and code review meaningful. Prefer OAuth over long-lived tokens.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Databricks Git folders \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/repos/\nDatabricks \u2014 CI/CD with Databricks Git folders \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/repos/ci-cd\nDatabricks \u2014 CI/CD on Databricks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/ci-cd-bundles",
        "optionA": "The credential grants access to the Databricks workspace rather than to the Git provider, which is why it is configured per user",
        "optionB": "No credential is needed for private repositories, because Unity Catalog privileges determine repository access",
        "optionC": "A single workspace-wide credential is used for all users, so every commit is attributed to the workspace's service account",
        "optionD": "The workspace stores a per-user Git credential so that each engineer's commits are attributed to their own provider identity and their access reflects their own repository permissions",
        "answer": [
            "optionD"
        ],
