        "question_no": "Q326",
        "question": "A customers table contains an ssn column and holds rows belonging to several regions. Compliance requires that: 1. Only members of the pii_readers group can see unmasked ssn values. 2. Regional analysts can see only the rows for their own region. Which approach satisfies both requirements without maintaining separate physical copies of the table?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "This question tests Unity Catalog's two fine-grained access controls, which restrict what a query returns based on who is asking.\nTwo Requirements, Two Controls\nThe requirements operate on different axes, and Unity Catalog provides one control for each:\nColumn masking \u2014 controls what a column returns. A mask is a SQL function applied to a column at query time, typically branching on group membership via is_account_group_member. Members of pii_readers receive real ssn values; everyone else receives a redacted value.\nRow filtering \u2014 controls which rows are returned. A row filter is a SQL function evaluated per row at query time, restricting each analyst to their own region.\nBoth attach to the table itself, so they apply on every access path \u2014 SQL editor, notebooks, BI tools, jobs. There is no route around them, which is what makes them a control rather than a convention.\nWhy This Beats Duplication\nOne table, one copy of the data, one place to change the policy. Adding a region requires no new object. And because the policy lives with the table, a new consumer inherits it automatically instead of needing to be told which copy to use.\nScaling Further\nApplying masks table by table works at small scale. Across hundreds of tables, ABAC policies let a mask or filter be defined centrally and applied wherever a matching tag appears \u2014 so newly created tables carrying a PII tag are covered without anyone remembering to act.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nLineage for auditing, \"masking cannot be applied to existing columns\" \u2014 the premise is false; masks can be applied to existing columns. More fundamentally, lineage and audit logs record what happened after the fact. They do not prevent access, and the requirement is prevention.\nOne table per region with ssn dropped \u2014 physical copies are exactly what the requirement rules out. Every new region means another table, the copies drift from the source, and dropping ssn per copy multiplies the pipelines that must stay correct.\nNotebook-enforced filtering \u2014 governance implemented in application code is bypassed by any other access path, and every new consumer must reimplement it. Controls belong on the object, not in each consumer.\nKey Takeaway\nColumn masks change what a column returns; row filters restrict which rows are returned. Both attach to the table and evaluate the caller at query time, so one table serves many audiences with no duplication. ABAC policies apply the same controls centrally at scale.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Row filters and column masks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/\nDatabricks \u2014 Manually apply row filters and column masks \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/manually-apply\nDatabricks \u2014 Attribute-based access control in Unity Catalog \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/",
        "optionA": "Revoke SELECT on the table and require all access to go through a notebook that filters rows in PySpark before displaying results",
