        "question_no": "Q327",
        "question": "A DataFrame has a column tags holding an array of strings, and a column matrix holding an array of arrays. The engineer needs one output row per individual tag, and separately needs matrix reduced to a single-level array without changing the row count. Which functions accomplish these two goals respectively?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "This question tests two array functions that sound similar but differ on one decisive axis: whether the row count changes.\nexplode \u2014 Changes the Row Count\nexplode is a generator. Given an array, it emits one row per element, repeating the other columns:\nbefore:  id=1, tags=[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"]\nafter:   id=1, tag=\"a\"\nid=1, tag=\"b\"\nid=1, tag=\"c\"\nOne row becomes three. This is how you normalize a nested collection into rows for joining, filtering, or aggregating per element.\nflatten \u2014 Preserves the Row Count\nflatten is a value transformation. Given an array of arrays, it removes one level of nesting within the same row:\nbefore:  id=1, matrix=[[1,2],[3,4]]\nafter:   id=1, matrix=[1,2,3,4]\nOne row stays one row. The value's shape changed; the DataFrame's cardinality did not.\nMatching Functions to Requirements\n\"One output row per individual tag\" \u2192 row count must increase \u2192 explode(tags).\n\"Single-level array without changing the row count\" \u2192 flatten(matrix).\nThe requirement language itself tells you which function to reach for.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nSwapped (flatten(tags) / explode(matrix)) \u2014 flatten never creates rows, so it cannot produce one row per tag; explode always creates rows, so it cannot preserve the count.\nexplode for both \u2014 exploding an array of arrays yields one row per inner array, leaving elements still nested one level down. It also changes the row count, which the second requirement forbids.\narray_contains / size \u2014 these describe arrays rather than restructure them. array_contains returns a boolean; size returns an element count. Neither changes shape or cardinality.\nKey Takeaway\nexplode generates rows \u2014 one per element. flatten removes one nesting level within a row and leaves the count unchanged. Decide by asking whether the row count should change.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 SQL language reference \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/\nDatabricks \u2014 Databricks for Python developers \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/languages/python\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Delta Lake in Databricks? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta",
        "optionA": "flatten(tags) emits one row per element; explode(matrix) removes one level of nesting without generating rows",
        "optionB": "explode(tags) emits one row per element; flatten(matrix) removes one level of nesting without generating rows",
        "optionC": "explode for both, because explode collapses nested arrays in place when the input is an array of arrays",
        "optionD": "array_contains(tags) emits one row per element; size(matrix) reduces the nesting level",
        "answer": [
            "optionB"
        ],
        "type": "single"
