        "question_no": "Q329",
        "question": "A team has implemented the medallion architecture. After the Silver table is built from Bronze, a junior engineer proposes deleting the Bronze data to save storage, arguing that the refined Silver table supersedes it. Which response best reflects the intent of the medallion architecture?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests whether you understand why the medallion architecture keeps all three layers, rather than merely being able to name them.\nThe Common Misconception\nMany people picture medallion as a conveyor belt: data enters at Bronze, is refined into Silver, refined again into Gold, and the earlier stages become disposable. That mental model is wrong, and it's exactly what the junior engineer in this scenario is proposing.\nThe layers coexist. Bronze, Silver, and Gold all persist, all at different levels of refinement, and the storage cost of keeping all three is accepted deliberately.\nWhat Bronze Actually Is\nBronze is the system of record for what the source sent. It holds raw, unmodified data as delivered \u2014 typically append-only, with ingestion metadata such as source file name and load timestamp.\nThe value of that is not sentimental. It buys three specific capabilities:\nReplay. When a defect is found in Silver's transformation logic \u2014 and eventually one always is \u2014 the fix is to correct the code and reprocess from Bronze. Without Bronze, you must ask the source system to resend, and it may no longer hold that data, may have changed, or may be a partner you cannot ask.\nAdaptation to new requirements. Silver's cleaning step discards what today's requirements deem irrelevant. When someone asks next quarter for a field Silver dropped, Bronze still has it. Delete Bronze and that field is gone permanently.\nAudit. When a regulator or executive asks how a Gold figure was derived, Bronze is the evidence of the input. It's also where you demonstrate that a data quality problem originated upstream rather than in your pipeline.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Bronze duplicates storage without benefit\" \u2014 this restates the conveyor-belt misconception. Object storage is inexpensive relative to the cost of an unreproducible pipeline. The duplication is the point.\n\"Replace Bronze with a view over Silver\" \u2014 a view can only surface what Silver contains. Every row Silver's cleaning logic dropped and every value it overwrote are unrecoverable. This preserves neither the raw data nor the ability to replay.\n\"Retain until Silver's first VACUUM\" \u2014 VACUUM on Silver removes data files Silver's own current version no longer references. It is storage reclamation on one table and says nothing about whether the raw source data is still needed. The two are unrelated.\nKey Takeaway\nBronze, Silver, and Gold coexist by design. Bronze preserves raw source data so downstream layers can be rebuilt, audited, or adapted to new requirements. Deleting it trades a small storage saving for the permanent loss of replayability.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Medallion architecture \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/medallion\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Delta Lake in Databricks? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/delta\nDatabricks \u2014 Remove unused data files with vacuum \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/operations/vacuum",
        "optionA": "Bronze should be replaced by a view over Silver, which preserves lineage without storing the raw data a second time",
        "optionB": "Bronze should be retained only until the first VACUUM on the Silver table completes, after which it becomes redundant",
        "optionC": "Bronze should be deleted, because data moves forward through the layers and retaining Bronze duplicates storage without benefit",
        "optionD": "Bronze should be retained, because it holds raw unmodified source data that allows Silver and Gold to be rebuilt or audited if transformation logic changes or a defect is discovered",
        "answer": [
            "optionD"
        ],
        "type": "single"
