        "question_no": "Q330",
        "question": "A task in a Lakeflow Job intermittently fails because a downstream API rate-limits requests. The engineer configures 3 retries with a 5-minute retry interval, and a 20-minute task timeout. Which statement describes the resulting behavior?",
        "Correct answer": "optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests how retry and timeout settings compose \u2014 they are independent controls operating at different scopes.\nWhat Each Setting Bounds\nTimeout bounds a single attempt. An attempt exceeding 20 minutes is terminated and counts as a failure.\nRetry policy governs what happens after a failure: wait 5 minutes, try again, up to 3 retries.\nBecause they operate at different scopes, they compose cleanly. Each attempt gets its own full 20 minutes; the retry interval sits between attempts.\nWorst case here: 4 attempts \u00d7 up to 20 minutes, plus 3 \u00d7 5-minute intervals \u2014 up to roughly 95 minutes before the task is finally marked failed. Worth calculating when a job has a delivery deadline.\nWhy This Pairing Suits Rate Limiting\nRate limiting is transient by definition \u2014 the throttle window resets. The delay is what makes the retry work: retrying instantly would hit the same limit, while a 5-minute pause gives the window time to clear. Meanwhile the timeout protects against an attempt hanging indefinitely on an unresponsive API, converting an unbounded stall into a bounded failure that the retry can then address.\nOne Default Worth Remembering\nFor most job configurations, the default is no retries at all. Retry behavior must be enabled deliberately \u2014 a task that \"should have retried\" but didn't is usually a task where nobody configured it.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Retries are disabled by a timeout\" \u2014 they are complementary, not conflicting, and configuring both is the recommended pattern for tasks with flaky external dependencies.\n\"The timeout applies across all attempts combined\" \u2014 a shared budget would mean the timeout silently caps the retry count, making the two settings interact in a way nobody configured. Each attempt gets the full timeout.\n\"Retries indefinitely\" \u2014 retries are bounded by the configured count. Unbounded retry would let a permanently broken task consume compute forever.\nKey Takeaway\nThe timeout bounds each attempt; the retry policy governs what happens after a failure. They compose: attempt (up to 20 min) \u2192 fail \u2192 wait 5 min \u2192 retry, up to 3 times. Default is no retries.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Configure and edit Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/configure-job\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/\nDatabricks \u2014 Notebook task for jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/tasks/notebook",
        "optionA": "The 20-minute timeout applies across all attempts combined, so retries stop once 20 minutes have elapsed in total",
        "optionB": "Retries are disabled by the presence of a timeout, because the two settings conflict",
        "optionC": "Each attempt is allowed its own 20-minute timeout, and a failed attempt waits 5 minutes before the next retry, up to 3 retries",
        "optionD": "The task retries indefinitely until it succeeds, because a retry interval was specified",
        "answer": [
            "optionC"
        ],
