        "question_no": "Q331",
        "question": "An engineer runs SELECT * FROM sales.customers in a notebook and the query resolves to a table they did not expect. Which explanation is most consistent with Unity Catalog's naming rules?",
        "Correct answer": "optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests Unity Catalog's three-level namespace and what happens when a reference omits a level.\nThe Namespace\nEvery securable is addressed as catalog.schema.table:\nCatalog \u2014 top-level grouping, commonly per environment or business unit\nSchema \u2014 logical grouping within a catalog\nTable / view / volume / function \u2014 the object\nA fully qualified name is unambiguous. Anything shorter is resolved from context.\nHow a Two-Level Reference Resolves\nsales.customers supplies schema and table but not catalog. Names resolve right to left, so customers is the table and sales the schema. The missing catalog is filled in from the current catalog \u2014 set by the session (USE CATALOG) or defaulting to the workspace's configured default.\nSo the query silently becomes <default_catalog>.sales.customers. If the default is dev_analytics and the engineer assumed prod_analytics, the same statement reads a different table with no error and no warning.\nWhy This Matters Beyond the Exam\nThis is a real production hazard. A notebook that works in one session reads different data in another because the current catalog differs. In shared and production code the remedy is straightforward: fully qualify names, or set USE CATALOG explicitly at the top of the notebook so the assumption is stated rather than inherited.\nNote the parallel with Q16 \u2014 a bundle deployed without -t uses the default target. Same failure shape: an unstated default resolving differently than assumed.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Searches every accessible catalog, returns first match\" \u2014 would make results depend on catalog ordering and on the caller's permissions, so two users running identical SQL could get different tables. Unity Catalog deliberately uses a single well-defined default instead.\n\"Two-level references are invalid; resolved against Hive metastore\" \u2014 two-level references are valid and common. The Hive metastore is reached explicitly through the hive_metastore catalog, not as a fallback.\n\"sales = catalog, customers = schema\" \u2014 reads left to right, which is backwards. And a SELECT against a schema is not valid syntax.\nKey Takeaway\nUnity Catalog uses catalog.schema.table, resolved right to left. A two-level reference takes its catalog from the session or workspace default \u2014 so fully qualify names in shared and production code.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Unity Catalog? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/\nDatabricks \u2014 Unity Catalog managed tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/managed\nDatabricks \u2014 Table types \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/types",
