        "question_no": "Q339",
        "question": "Two Unity Catalog tables hold the same data. sales.mgd_orders is a managed table, and sales.ext_orders is an external table whose LOCATION points at an S3 prefix. An engineer runs DROP TABLE on both. What happens to the underlying data files?",
        "Correct answer": "optionB",
        "explanation": "This question tests the defining difference between managed and external tables: who owns the storage lifecycle.\nThe Distinction\nManaged tables \u2014 Unity Catalog chooses and owns the storage location, drawn from managed storage configured at the metastore, catalog, or schema level. Unity Catalog handles read, write, storage, and optimization responsibilities.\nExternal tables \u2014 you supply a LOCATION pointing at cloud storage you own. Unity Catalog governs access to that data, but not the storage itself.\nWhat DROP TABLE Does to Each\nFor the managed table, dropping removes both metadata and data. Databricks deletes the underlying files after a recovery period \u2014 7 days by default, during which UNDROP TABLE can recover it. After that the files are removed from cloud storage.\nFor the external table, dropping unregisters it from Unity Catalog and leaves every file in place. Cleaning up is your responsibility.\nWhy This Design Is Right\nExternal tables exist precisely because a storage location may be shared \u2014 other engines, other teams, other pipelines may read the same path. If DROP TABLE deleted a customer-owned location's contents, unregistering a table from one catalog could destroy data another system depends on. Databricks does not assume ownership of storage you nominated.\nConversely, managed tables are recommended by default: reduced storage and compute costs, automatic maintenance, and predictive optimization all depend on Unity Catalog owning the lifecycle.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\n\"Neither is deleted; use VACUUM\" \u2014 true for external, false for managed. VACUUM is also the wrong instrument: it removes files unreferenced by a live table, and after a DROP there is no table for it to act on.\n\"Both are deleted\" \u2014 this would make external tables unusable for shared data, which is their main purpose.\n\"Managed files remain until retention expires; external files deleted immediately by the cloud provider\" \u2014 inverts the behavior. Databricks issues no instruction that would cause a provider to delete an external location's contents.\nKey Takeaway\nDROP TABLE on a managed table deletes the data (recoverable via UNDROP for ~7 days). On an external table it removes only the registration; the files remain in your storage.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Unity Catalog managed tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/managed\nDatabricks \u2014 External tables \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/external\nDatabricks \u2014 Table types \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/types",
        "optionA": "The managed table's files remain until its retention period expires, while the external table's files are deleted immediately by the cloud provider",
        "optionB": "The managed table's data files are deleted along with its metadata, while the external table's metadata is removed but its files remain in S3",
        "optionC": "Neither table's data files are deleted - DROP TABLE removes only metadata, and files must be cleared separately with VACUUM",
        "optionD": "Both tables' data files are deleted, because Unity Catalog governs the storage for every table type",
