        "question_no": "Q342",
        "question": "An Auto Loader stream reads JSON files. The engineer finds that the price and in_stock fields have both landed in the target table as STRING, although price should be a double and in_stock a boolean. Which TWO actions would cause these fields to land with their intended types? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionA, optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests why JSON fields arrive as strings, and the two legitimate remedies.\nThe Root Cause\nJSON carries no type information. A file may show \"price\": 24.99 and \"in_stock\": true, but those are syntactic hints, not declarations. Auto Loader's conservative default is to treat every JSON field as STRING \u2014 safe, because it never guesses wrong and never fails on an unexpected value, but it pushes casting downstream.\nRemedy 1 \u2014 Supply an Explicit Schema\nDeclare the schema on the reader. Auto Loader stops inferring and applies your types. This is the most deterministic option: the types cannot drift when a later batch of files looks different, which matters for columns downstream logic depends on.\nThe cost is maintenance \u2014 a new source column requires a schema change.\nRemedy 2 \u2014 Enable cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes\nThis tells Auto Loader to examine the data and assign DOUBLE, BOOLEAN, LONG, and so on rather than defaulting to string. Convenient on wide or evolving sources.\nThe caveat worth knowing: inference is sampled. If early files always show integers and a later one contains a decimal, the inferred type may be wrong. For critical columns, prefer an explicit schema \u2014 or combine inference with schema hints, which override specific columns while leaving the rest inferred.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nmergeSchema \u2014 reconciles differing schemas by adding new columns. It does not change the type of an existing column, and narrowing string to double is not a merge-compatible evolution.\nschemaEvolutionMode set to rescue \u2014 evolution modes govern what happens when the incoming schema differs from the known one. Rescue mode captures data that doesn't fit into a rescued data column; it preserves unexpected values rather than retyping existing ones.\nVACUUM \u2014 deletes data files the current version no longer references. It never rewrites live data and has no relationship to schema or types.\nKey Takeaway\nJSON is untyped, so Auto Loader defaults to STRING. Fix it with an explicit schema (deterministic) or cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes (convenient, sampled). Schema hints override individual columns.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Auto Loader? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader\nDatabricks \u2014 Schema enforcement \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/schema-enforcement\nDatabricks \u2014 Update table schemas with schema evolution \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/update-schema",
        "optionA": "Supply an explicit schema to the reader so that Auto Loader does not have to infer types from the JSON",
        "optionB": "Enable mergeSchema on the write, which retypes the existing string columns to match the source",
        "optionC": "Run VACUUM on the target table, which rewrites its files using the inferred schema",
        "optionD": "Enable cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes so that Auto Loader infers types rather than defaulting JSON fields to string",
        "optionE": "Set cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode to rescue, which coerces string columns to their most specific type",
        "answer": [
            "optionA",
            "optionD"
        ],
        "type": "multi"
