        "question_no": "Q345",
        "question": "A team must run a pipeline whenever a partner uploads a file to cloud storage, which happens at unpredictable times between 1 a.m. and 6 a.m. They also want a guaranteed run at 7 a.m. even if no file arrived overnight. Which TWO Lakeflow Jobs trigger configurations together satisfy this? (Choose two.)",
        "Correct answer": "optionB, optionC",
        "explanation": "This question tests trigger types, and specifically that data-driven and time-based triggers solve different problems \u2014 so a requirement naming both needs both.\nTwo Requirements, Two Triggers\n\"Run when a file arrives, at unpredictable times\" \u2014 data-driven. A file arrival trigger monitors a cloud storage location and starts the job when new files appear. The job reacts to the partner's actual timing instead of guessing.\n\"Guaranteed run at 7 a.m. even if no file arrived\" \u2014 time-based. A scheduled (cron) trigger fires regardless.\nThat second requirement is easy to skim past, but it's doing important work. It is a dead-man's switch. Without it, a night when the partner sends nothing looks identical to a night when everything worked \u2014 no run, no failure, no alert, and nobody notices until someone asks why yesterday's numbers are missing. The 7 a.m. run turns silent absence into a visible event.\nCombining Triggers\nA job can carry more than one trigger. Pairing a data-driven trigger with a scheduled backstop is a standard production pattern: react promptly when data arrives, and verify daily that it did.\nIf both fire, concurrency settings control what happens \u2014 typically limiting to one concurrent run so the 7 a.m. run is skipped or queued when a file-triggered run is still going.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nTable update trigger \u2014 fires when a Unity Catalog table is written. The partner writes a file to storage, which is a different event. It would respond to a downstream effect rather than the upstream cause, and cannot start the job that performs the write.\nContinuous trigger \u2014 keeps compute alive through hours of inactivity for a once-nightly delivery. It also provides no guaranteed daily checkpoint; a permanently running job has no notion of \"7 a.m. happened.\"\nManual trigger in a runbook \u2014 replaces automation with a person watching storage overnight. Unreliable, unscalable, and unnecessary given that file arrival triggers exist.\nKey Takeaway\nCombine a file arrival trigger (react to unpredictable data) with a scheduled cron trigger (guaranteed daily run, surfacing missed deliveries). Data-driven and time-based triggers answer different questions.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/\nDatabricks \u2014 Configure and edit Lakeflow Jobs \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/configure-job\nDatabricks \u2014 Set up incremental ingestion from Amazon S3 \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/onboard-data",
        "optionA": "A manual trigger documented in a runbook, which the on-call engineer fires after seeing a file land",
        "optionB": "A file arrival trigger on the storage location, which starts the job when new files appear",
        "optionC": "A scheduled (cron) trigger at 7 a.m., which runs the job regardless of whether a file arrived",
        "optionD": "A continuous trigger, which keeps the job running so that it picks up files as they land and also satisfies the daily requirement",
        "optionE": "A table update trigger on the target table, which fires whenever the partner's file is written to storage",
        "answer": [
            "optionB",
            "optionC"
        ],
        "type": "multi"
