        "question_no": "Q346",
        "question": "An Auto Loader stream ingests from an S3 prefix that now holds 4.2 million files, and each micro-batch spends several minutes before any data is processed. The team has permission to provision cloud resources. Which change addresses the bottleneck, and what is the trade-off?",
        "Correct answer": "optionD",
        "explanation": "This question tests Auto Loader's two file discovery modes and the scale at which the default stops working.\nThe Two Modes\nDirectory listing (default) \u2014 enumerates the source location to find files it hasn't seen. Simple, needs no extra infrastructure, works immediately.\nFile notification \u2014 subscribes to the cloud provider's storage event service. New files are announced rather than discovered.\nWhy 4.2 Million Files Breaks Listing\nListing cost scales with the total number of files present, not with how many are new. At 4.2 million files, every micro-batch enumerates all of them to find the handful that arrived. That is why minutes pass before any data is processed \u2014 the stream isn't slow at processing, it's slow at looking.\nFile notification inverts the relationship. Cost becomes proportional to arrivals, so a directory holding 4 million or 40 million files makes no difference.\nThe Trade-Off Stated Honestly\nNotification mode requires cloud infrastructure: a notification configuration and a queue in the storage account's cloud, plus credentials permitted to create or consume them. That setup and its ongoing permissions are the price of the scalability \u2014 which is exactly why directory listing remains the sensible default for modest directories.\nWhy the other options are incorrect\nMove files to a managed volume \u2014 a volume is a governance abstraction over a cloud storage location, not a separately indexed file system. Reading from a volume path still requires the same discovery, so the bottleneck follows the files.\nRaise cloudFiles.maxFilesPerTrigger \u2014 this caps how many files a micro-batch processes. It does not change how files are discovered; the listing still enumerates the prefix every time.\nReplace with COPY INTO \u2014 it also identifies candidate files at the source location, facing the same enumeration cost. COPY INTO suits periodic idempotent loads, not listing overhead at this scale.\nSwitch notification \u2192 listing \u2014 backwards. It moves from the scalable mode to the one already failing.\nKey Takeaway\nDirectory listing cost scales with total files present; file notification cost scales with arrivals. At millions of files, switch to notification mode \u2014 accepting the cloud notification and queue infrastructure it requires.\nOfficial Documentation References\nDatabricks \u2014 What is Auto Loader? \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader\nDatabricks \u2014 Set up incremental ingestion from Amazon S3 \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/onboard-data\nDatabricks \u2014 Production considerations for Structured Streaming \u2192 https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/production",
        "optionA": "Keep directory listing but raise cloudFiles.maxFilesPerTrigger, which removes the need to list the directory",
        "optionB": "Replace Auto Loader with COPY INTO, which does not need to enumerate the source location",
        "optionC": "Switch from file notification to directory listing mode, which avoids the latency of cloud event delivery - at the cost of broader permissions",
        "optionD": "Switch from directory listing to file notification mode, which uses cloud event services to detect new files instead of enumerating the prefix - at the cost of additional cloud infrastructure setup",
        "optionE": "Move the files into a Unity Catalog managed volume, which eliminates file discovery because volumes maintain their own file index",
        "answer": [
            "optionD"
        ],
